window.ENG = {"headline":"Start with the animals backend. It holds the one high-severity finding.\n\n- It now sends the status SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED to PIMS.\n- It sends it on events whose published schemas allow only SUBMITTED.\n\nNext, in the same area, look at the cancel-amend status. It can disagree with the documents held.\n\nThen look at the plants confirmation date.\n\n- It is wrong by a day for the first hour after midnight in summer.\n- This is because the backend saves a time with no time zone.\n- The tests were widened to accept the wrong day, so they hide it.\n\nThird, look at the workspace build gate. It can take down the stack and delete the lease that the run itself holds.\n\nIn the frontends, begin with the backend fetch timeout and retry change:\n\n- it does nothing useful on CDP\n- it splits backend calls across two fetch paths\n- only the local test paths exercise it\n\nThe same area also covers the raised FIT timeout.\n\nThen check that:\n\n- subdivision codes picked as transit countries reach GBN-AG as raw country codes\n- the INS frontend still drops fetch causes from its logs\n- the plants Check your answers links send a trader round in a loop\n\nThe remaining findings are low severity:\n\n- test strength\n- stub provenance\n- build-loop edge cases\n- comments\n- accessibility","reviewed":11,"refuted":7,"order":[{"rank":1,"area":"Animals backend","priority":1,"why":"It holds the only high-severity finding: a status value that breaks the published GBN-AG contract to PIMS.\n\nIt also has a stale-status defect on cancel-amend."},{"rank":2,"area":"Tests repo","priority":1,"why":"Its date checks were widened to hide a live one-day-off product bug.\n\nSeveral other assertions are looser than they should be."},{"rank":3,"area":"Workspace tooling","priority":1,"why":"The build gate can delete the run's own stack lease after Docker Desktop restarts.\n\nThat affects lease safety and run continuity."},{"rank":4,"area":"Performance tests","priority":1,"why":"The k6 walk drifts slightly from the real opening run.\n\nIt also logs a failure line on every iteration, including the sign-in callback URL."},{"rank":5,"area":"Stub and reference-data","priority":1,"why":"The code is sound. What remains:\n\n- fixture provenance\n- overclaimed stub fidelity\n- test hygiene"},{"rank":6,"area":"Backend fetch timeout and retry","priority":2,"why":"It must head the frontend list. The change:\n\n- does nothing on CDP\n- forks the fetch path\n- is tested through a library mock\n- sits beside a raised FIT timeout"},{"rank":7,"area":"Animals frontend shared code","priority":2,"why":"Subdivision codes now chosen as transit and destination countries reach GBN-AG unmapped.\n\nDuring an amendment, 'Save and return' behaves differently from page to page."},{"rank":8,"area":"INS frontend","priority":2,"why":"The new cause logging misses:\n\n- the countries 503\n- every error caught inside a controller\n\nSo the diagnostic gap it set out to close is still open."},{"rank":9,"area":"Animals frontend feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"why":"It repeats the subdivision-to-GBN-AG risk.\n\nIt adds smaller contradictions in titles, accessibility and comments."},{"rank":10,"area":"Plants frontend feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"why":"Check your answers error links can trap a trader in a loop.\n\nAlso open:\n\n- a Welsh date\n- a cross-feature import\n- comment hygiene"}],"areas":[{"key":"backend","title":"Animals backend","priority":1,"summary":"I reviewed all of origin/main...HEAD in trade-imports-animals-backend (PR #107, 4 commits).\n\nI also followed the new status downstream into:\n\n- the outbox\n- trade-imports-dynamics-gateway\n- trade-imports-ins-backend\n- the GBN-AG schemas in trade-imports-schemas\n\nInside the service the change holds up. These are all sound:\n\n- status guards\n- null handling on cancel-amend for older records\n- the projection fields\n- the unit coverage\n\nThe serious problem is the wire contract.\n\n- Every submit without a scanned ITAHC now sends notificationStatusCode SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED to PIMS.\n- It sends it on events whose declared schemas pin it to the constant SUBMITTED.\n- trade-imports-schemas was not changed.\n\nA second, medium defect: cancel-amend restores a stored status that can disagree with the documents actually held.","survived":3,"killed":0},{"key":"stub-refdata","title":"Stub and reference-data","priority":1,"summary":"I reviewed both changes against origin/main.\n\n- The stub (inc-173): controllers now serve captured MDM JSON as-is, the six DTOs are deleted, and there are new fixtures and tests.\n- reference-data (inc-007): PortType, PortListOrder, the traffic mapping and its tests.\n\nI checked the fixtures with jq against the raw CDP-test captures and against every consumer.\n\nThe code is sound. The fixtures match the captures:\n\n- 250 countries\n- 32 in the GBNAG_SPS_EX block\n- 78 ports whose traffic is all Airport, Port or Rail, as in the raw capture\n\nreference-data's model still reads the new shape. Nothing else reads the stub's old fields.\n\nWhat remains is one fixture-provenance gap and three smaller risks.\n\nNone of them is a runtime defect today.","survived":4,"killed":0},{"key":"tests-repo","title":"Tests repo","priority":1,"summary":"I reviewed every change in trade-imports-ins-tests from origin/main...HEAD (124 files, 61 commits).\n\nI compared the assertions in each changed spec against main. I also reviewed the page objects, flows, seeding helpers and commit 65b6afc.\n\nMost spec changes follow the Design Release 2.1 rulings. Many assertions are stricter than before:\n\n- exact toHaveText where main used toContainText\n- full innerText address checks\n- new states for Amend, Submitted and Submitted action required\n\nNone of the changes made to beat a flake dropped or weakened an assertion. Those changes were:\n\n- the test.slow() calls\n- the seeded pagination spec\n- the reordered beforeEach in the read-only address-book specs\n\nThe real risks are elsewhere:\n\n- date checks that now accept the wrong calendar day\n- an INS dashboard test that locks in a raw status enum shown to users\n- one assertion that can never fail\n- a page-object heading that now matches several pages\n- reference-list predicates left loose after the themes were merged\n\nCommit 65b6afc is superseded and not needed.","survived":4,"killed":2},{"key":"perf","title":"Performance tests","priority":1,"summary":"I reviewed the whole trade-imports-performance-tests diff (origin/main...HEAD, 3 commits, 7 files).\n\nI compared it against the live-animals frontend's new RUN_STEPS and flow.js on the combined branch.\n\nThe import-reason move is correct. The first six k6 steps now match Design Release 2.1.\n\nTwo gaps remain:\n\n- After additional-details, the k6 walk still does not follow the real opening run.\n- The change missed the frontend dropping cph-number from the run.\n\nNeither shows up in any check. This is because walker.reach quietly opens a page directly when the previous save landed somewhere else.\n\nNo threshold or measurement was weakened.\n\nThe new missed-page logging is useful, but:\n\n- it only covers the INS dashboard\n- it logs on every iteration, including the OIDC callback URL","survived":2,"killed":0},{"key":"workspace-tooling","title":"Workspace tooling","priority":1,"summary":"I reviewed the workspace tooling diff (3c28ff5a~1..HEAD) across:\n\n- tim exec (stack, stack-lease, stack-refresh, stack-fingerprints, docker-daemon)\n- tim build gate\n- tim backlog split, split-off and themes\n- tim distil\n- the increment-build-loop workflow\n\nThe backlog split-off, relink, distil and x-refresh-with code is sound and well tested.\n\nThe main defect:\n\n- When Docker Desktop has quit, tim now starts it again inside every `docker ps`.\n- The gate then treats the run's own down stack as one it started itself.\n- It releases that stack after the e2e rungs, which takes it down and deletes the run's lease.\n- So the new recover-and-re-lease path almost never runs.\n\nA second risk: the new rollback step in the failed-attempt handler soft-resets every unpushed commit in each repo. It does this for all of them, not just the failed attempt's.\n\nNeither lets wrong code land green.\n\nBoth affect lease safety and keeping the branch's history intact.","survived":5,"killed":0},{"key":"backend-fetch","title":"Backend fetch timeout and retry","priority":2,"summary":"I reviewed:\n\n- backend-fetch.js and its tests\n- the vitest set-up shim\n- the errors.js cause logging\n\nThe animals and plants frontends carry the same file (blob d1664a3 in both).\n\nI also reviewed the animals FIT timeout raise. I checked both against the build's stack logs and the GitHub Actions runs.\n\nOn CDP the change does nothing useful. HTTP_PROXY is set there, so:\n\n- the 2s bound never applies\n- the retry almost never fires\n\nIt also splits backend calls into two fetch/FormData paths. No test environment now runs the CDP one.\n\nIt was tested through a library mock that hides what it sends.\n\nMy verdict is to remove it from production code, keeping stackWithCauses.\n\nThe FIT timeout raise covers real set-up cost, not flakes (0 retries).","survived":5,"killed":0},{"key":"animals-shared","title":"Animals frontend shared code","priority":2,"summary":"I reviewed every file the build changed in trade-imports-animals-frontend outside the feature folders:\n\n- shared kit\n- save-actions\n- amend-form-actions\n- the lib/validate soft save\n- the navigation change from nextInSection to nextInTaskRow\n- run.js steps and Back targets\n- the entry guard\n- task rows and flow sections\n- the new SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED status in records\n- the real and stub marshal/write guards and lifecycle\n- the countries and ports services\n- errors.js\n- context.js\n- the FIT changes and the test setup\n\nbackend-fetch.js was skipped as asked.\n\nMost of it is sound:\n\n- Submit is still gated on readyForCheckYourAnswers, so a soft save cannot submit invalid data.\n- isSubmittedStatus is used everywhere a status is checked.\n- Write guards still block submitted records.\n- The reordered sections leave the ENFORCED_AT_CONTINUE prerequisites unchanged.\n- Only live-animals is under sets/, so there is no germinal set for the nextTarget change to alter.\n- I found no navigation loops or dead ends.\n\nThe one finding worth reviewing hard is the new subdivision codes reaching GBN-AG unmapped.","survived":2,"killed":1},{"key":"plants-shared","title":"Plants frontend shared code","priority":2,"summary":"I reviewed every plants-frontend change outside the per-page feature folders, on feat/NO_JIRA-design-release-2-1-combined against origin/main.\n\nThat covered:\n\n- validate allowMissing\n- nextInTaskRow\n- the opening run now ending on check your answers\n- the entry guard's not-found pass-through\n- amendFormActions with the amend save-actions macro\n- the journey strip's Cancel amend link\n- page titles\n- port option text\n- stackWithCauses\n- the vitest undici setup file\n\nI compared each against the animals copy.\n\nThis shared code is largely sound:\n\n- run.js, navigation.js, amend-form-actions.js, context.js and save-actions.njk match animals, except for the plants-only besidePrimary.\n- The targeted unit suites pass (19 files, 394 tests).\n\nI found no correctness defect.\n\nThere are two low findings:\n\n- an unbounded loop over error causes in the new error logging\n- one gap in soft save between the two frontends that someone needs to confirm was intended","survived":0,"killed":2},{"key":"ins-frontend","title":"INS frontend","priority":2,"summary":"I reviewed the whole trade-imports-ins-frontend diff from origin/main to HEAD (7 commits, 26 files).\n\n- The page-title change follows the GOV.UK pattern in both English and Welsh. Its tests are sound.\n- The SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED link matches inc-113 and the animals backend's status enum.\n- The countries stub now loads from a captured fixture. GB is still added by address-countries.js.\n- The capture script is a harmless dev script.\n\nThe real problem is errors.js.\n\nIts cause-chain logging logs no secrets or personal data. But it misses most of the failures it was written to explain.\n\nTwo weaker points remain:\n\n- a cause chain with no end\n- a raw status enum shown to users","survived":3,"killed":2},{"key":"animals-features","title":"Animals frontend feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"summary":"I sampled the live-animals feature folders in trade-imports-animals-frontend (diff against origin/main).\n\nThe sample covered:\n\n- check-answers (controller, template, view-header, species-errors, incomplete-cards, documents, commodity cards)\n- import-reason\n- origin\n- transit-countries\n- commodities/search\n- confirmation\n- cancel-amend\n- their unit and fit tests\n\nThe area is mostly clean:\n\n- Nothing in the diff touches the obligation model.\n- No new vi.mock or spy calls appear in the feature tests.\n- The fit specs walk the journey rather than seed it. Seeding happens only in the in-memory unit tests.\n- No assertion was weakened to beat a flake. A few long walks had their timeout raised to 90s.\n\nThere are seven findings. The two that matter most:\n\n- a cross-repo risk: subdivision codes now saved as transit countries go to GBN-AG as country codes\n- an inconsistency between pages in whether 'Save and return' during an amendment skips the required-answer checks\n\nThe rest are low:\n\n- the draft review's page title contradicts an earlier acceptance criterion\n- a maxlength attribute conflicts with the prefix-forgiving parser\n- a new cross-feature import of a controller\n- region landmarks on the hub\n- explanatory comments added to source","survived":7,"killed":0},{"key":"plants-features","title":"Plants frontend feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"summary":"I reviewed the diff against origin/main for the sampled plants feature folders:\n\n- check-answers (controller, view-model, outstanding-rows, summary rows, template, copy, tests)\n- arrival-details\n- origin\n- commodity-type\n- declaration\n- cancel-amend\n\nI also read the shared helpers they now depend on: validate allowMissing, kit.nextTarget/exitTarget, save-actions and amend-form-actions.\n\nOne real behaviour defect is worth close review:\n\n- Check your answers now lists one error per unfinished task row.\n- But each error links to the row's first page with ?change=1.\n- Saving there goes straight back to Check your answers.\n- So a row that is half done traps the trader in a loop.\n- A unit test fixes that link in place.\n\nThe other findings are low.\n\nTests drive the UI rather than seeding journeys. The only mocks are the address-book and records boundaries.\n\nThe markup stays within GOV.UK Frontend components. The new HTML address rows escape their input.","survived":4,"killed":0}],"findings":[{"rank":1,"ranks":[1],"id":"animals-backend-f1","area":"Animals backend","priority":1,"title":"SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED sent to PIMS on events whose schemas require SUBMITTED","repo":"trade-imports-animals-backend","file":"src/main/java/uk/gov/defra/trade/imports/animals/notification/NotificationService.java","line":210,"what":"submitNotification and cancelAmendNotification now write the aggregate with status SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED.\n\nOutboxService then serialises it unchanged into two places:\n- data.exchangedDocument.notificationStatusCode (through ExchangedDocument.from, status.name())\n- statusChanges[].status\n\nThe events are NotificationSubmitted, NotificationSubmissionAmended and NotificationAmendmentCancelled. Each carries a schemaUrl.\n\nThe schemas those URLs name all pin the value with {\"const\": \"SUBMITTED\"}. They are trade-imports-schemas gbn-ag-event-notification-submitted-v1, -submission-amended-v1 and -amendment-cancelled-v1.\n\nThe GBN-AG status codelist (codelists/gbn-ag-notification-status.json) and gbn-ag-state-transitions.md know only DRAFT, SUBMITTED, AMEND and DELETED.\n\ntrade-imports-schemas is still on main. It has no change for the new status.\n\nThe dynamics gateway passes the string straight through to PIMS:\n- PimsGbnAgDataMapper/V2 pass doc.notificationStatusCode() through\n- OutboxStatusChange.status is a String\n\nSam's add-only ruling (claim 42) mapped the new status in the animals frontend and the INS frontend. It did not map it in the external GBN-AG/PIMS contract.\n\nNotificationIT line 938 now pins the non-conforming value as expected behaviour. Four older outbox ITs were changed from SUBMITTED to SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED.","scenario":"A trader submits a live-animals notification without a scanned ITAHC. The ITs show this is now the default for any notification with no document.\n\nWhat happens next:\n- The outbox writes a NotificationSubmitted event. It declares schemaUrl gbn-ag-event-notification-submitted-v1.schema.json but carries notificationStatusCode \"SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED\".\n- The gateway forwards it to PIMS over Azure Service Bus.\n- PIMS either fails schema validation, or does not recognise the code and does not treat the notification as submitted.\n- The BCP and caseworkers then never see a pre-notification the trader believes was submitted.\n\nEach later amend, cancel-amend or resubmit repeats the problem.","severity":"high","category":"cross-feature","fix":"Do not ship until the wire contract is settled.\n\nOption 1: keep the GBN-AG wire status as SUBMITTED.\n- Map SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED to SUBMITTED in ExchangedDocument.from and when building StatusChange.\n- Carry the outstanding items as their own field if downstream needs them.\n- This also means the INS backend needs another way to show 'action required'. NotificationUpsertService reads status only from notificationStatusCode.\n\nOption 2: add the code to the gbn-ag codelist and minted v2 event schemas in trade-imports-schemas.\n- Agree it with the PIMS team.\n- Make the change on the same branch name.\n\nEither way, add an IT asserting a clean-ITAHC submit's event carries notificationStatusCode SUBMITTED.","verifierNote":"Confirmed on the branch.\n\n- NotificationStatus gains SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED (diff).\n- submitNotification (NotificationService.java:202-212) uses it whenever OutstandingItem.outstandingFor finds no COMPLETE ITAHC or HEALTH_CERTIFICATE. That covers no upload, and also an ITAHC whose scan is still PENDING at submit.\n- cancelAmendNotification (line 288) can restore it too.\n- ExchangedDocument.from (outbox/gbnag/ExchangedDocument.java:32) writes status.name() into notificationStatusCode.\n- OutboxService:74-76 appends it to statusChanges.\n- OutboxEventType points the schemaUrl at gbn-ag-event-notification-submitted-v1, -submission-amended-v1 and -amendment-cancelled-v1.\n- In trade-imports-schemas all three pin exchangedDocument.notificationStatusCode to {\"const\": \"SUBMITTED\"} (checked submitted-v1 line 26). That repo has no commits on the branch.\n- The dynamics gateway's PimsGbnAgDataMapper and PimsGbnAgDataMapperV2 pass the string through unchanged.\n\nSam's ruling claim 42 lists only the animals frontend, the INS frontend and the stub.\n\nHis earlier ruling (point 8, 2026-10-09) says no PIMS profile or schema change is made in this programme.\n\nSo the new value contradicts the declared contract and the stated scope. NotificationIT:938 pins the non-conforming value as correct.\n\nWhether PIMS actually rejects the event is unproven. But sending it breaches the declared contract, so high stands.","also":[]},{"rank":2,"ranks":[2],"id":"animals-backend-f2","area":"Animals backend","priority":1,"title":"Cancel-amend restores a stale outstanding-items status while document changes made during the amendment stay","repo":"trade-imports-animals-backend","file":"src/main/java/uk/gov/defra/trade/imports/animals/notification/NotificationService.java","line":288,"what":"cancelAmendNotification sets the status from the outstandingItems kept from the last submission.\n\nAccompanying documents live in their own collection:\n- an upload made during the amendment is not rolled back on cancel\n- a deletion (DocumentService.deleteByUploadId, a hard delete) is not restored\n\nOnly notification content and fulfilments are restored.\n\nThe restored status can therefore contradict:\n- the documents the notification holds\n- the documents the AmendmentCancelled event lists in referenceDocument (OutboxService reads the live COMPLETE documents)\n\nThe designed path for clearing 'action required' is Continue, which starts an amendment (inc-113, c-144). So traders will take this path.","scenario":"Case A\n\n- A notification is SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED.\n- The trader clicks Continue (amend), uploads an ITAHC that scans COMPLETE, then clicks Cancel amend.\n- The status reverts to SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED and the dashboard warns that the certificate is missing.\n- Meanwhile the event's referenceDocument lists the clean ITAHC.\n\nCase B\n\n- A notification is SUBMITTED with an ITAHC.\n- The trader amends, deletes the ITAHC, then cancels.\n- The status becomes SUBMITTED with outstandingItems [], but no health certificate exists.\n- PIMS receives SUBMITTED with no ITAHC reference.","severity":"medium","category":"correctness","fix":"Recompute on cancel as submit does:\n- call OutstandingItem.outstandingFor(documentService.findByNotificationRef(referenceNumber))\n- then set both outstandingItems and the status from that result\n\nAlternatively, snapshot the accompanying-document set at amend and restore it on cancel.\n\nAdd an IT for the amend, upload ITAHC, cancel-amend path.","verifierNote":"Confirmed.\n\ncancelAmendNotification (line 288) takes the status from the outstandingItems stored at the last submit. It never re-reads the documents.\n\nNothing under accompanyingdocument/ mentions amend or cancel. Uploads and deletes have no notification-status guard.\n\nSo documents added or removed during an amendment survive a cancel, while the status reverts. Meanwhile OutboxService:91 lists the live COMPLETE documents in the event.\n\nCase A is the realistic one.\n\nThe designed recovery is amend, upload the ITAHC, then submit. A trader who uploads and then cancels stays at action-required with a clean ITAHC on file.\n\nThe documents not rolling back is older behaviour. The stale status that contradicts them is new in this diff.\n\nMedium is right.","also":[]},{"rank":3,"ranks":[3],"id":"tests-repo-f1","area":"Tests repo","priority":1,"title":"Date checks accept yesterday at any hour, hiding an off-by-one-day bug","repo":"trade-imports-ins-tests","file":"tests/plants/e2e/features/review.spec.ts","line":201,"what":"Commit 3e7b227 widened the plants confirmation 'Date of notification' check to accept yesterday, today or tomorrow.\n\nThese checks accept yesterday or today:\n- the animals view's 'Date created' and 'Date submitted' checks (notification-view-states.spec.ts:40, 183, 266)\n- the plants ones (review.spec.ts:412, 629)\n\nThe commit says the extra day is only needed in the hour after midnight BST. But the tests accept it at every hour of the day.\n\nThe plants backend stores submittedAt as a LocalDateTime with no zone (NotificationAggregate.java:50). The confirmation controller reads it with new Date(value) before formatting for Europe/London.\n\nThe date shown is therefore only right when the frontend process runs in UTC. The tests cannot catch it being wrong.","scenario":"The plants frontend runs with TZ=Europe/London (native dev, or a container setting changes).\n\n- At 14:00 a trader submits on 12 October.\n- The zone-less submittedAt is read as London time, so a UTC-to-London regression can push the date a day off.\n- If the page shows 11 October, review.spec.ts:201 and :412 still pass, because yesterday is in the accepted set.\n\nThe same applies to the animals 'Date submitted' header.","severity":"medium","category":"test-weakened","fix":"Do one of these:\n- accept the previous day only when the London clock is between 00:00 and 01:00 (compute the expected set from the current time)\n- or read submittedAt back through the API and format it in Europe/London to get one expected string\n\nSeparately, raise a ticket for the plants backend to serialise submittedAt as an Instant (or with an offset). That way the frontend's new Date() does not depend on the process timezone.","verifierNote":"Confirmed.\n\n- review.spec.ts:201 accepts -1/0/+1 days.\n- :412, :629, plus notification-view-states.spec.ts:40, 183, 266, accept -1/0.\n\nCommit 3e7b227 says the extra day is only needed in the hour after midnight BST. But the tests accept it at every hour.\n\nThe reviewer's scenario understates the problem. The bug is live, not hypothetical:\n- The plants frontend Dockerfile already sets ENV TZ=\"Europe/London\" (Dockerfile:9, :34).\n- The backend stamps submittedAt with zone-less LocalDateTime.now() (NotificationService.java:236, NotificationAggregate.java:50).\n- confirmation/controller.js:25 parses it with new Date(value).\n- A zone-less ISO date-time is parsed as process-local (London) time, so the page shows the backend JVM's UTC wall date.\n- Between 00:00 and 01:00 BST it shows yesterday.\n\nThe build widened the test to hide a real product bug. The controller's own comment ('submittedAt is a UTC instant') is wrong.\n\nFix:\n- serialise submittedAt and created as an Instant or with an offset (or parse with an explicit Z)\n- then tighten the tests to today only, or to a set computed from the London clock","also":[]},{"rank":4,"ranks":[4],"id":"workspace-tooling-f1","area":"Workspace tooling","priority":1,"title":"Gate tears down and deletes the run's own lease after Docker Desktop quits","repo":"trade-imports-workspace","file":"tim/src/build/gate.js","line":429,"what":"psStackWithDocker (tim/src/exec/stack.js:159) now starts Docker Desktop again whenever docker ps says the daemon is unreachable.\n\nIn the gate:\n- takeLease calls acquireStack as RUN_HOLDER.\n- The run's lease is state UP, and Docker came back with no containers (the stack sets no restart policies).\n- So acquireDecision returns 'start', startUnderLease rebuilds the stack, and lease.started is true.\n- runE2e's finally block then calls giveBack.\n- releaseStack runs stop-stack.sh and removes the lease file.\n\nThe gate cannot tell 'I started a free stack' apart from 'I restarted my own run's stack'.\n\nBefore this change, an unreachable daemon made takeLease fail. The stage reported it, and the new recoverDownStack in the loop would have taken the lease again.","scenario":"An overnight branch run holds the lease as ibl-…, and a Homebrew cask upgrade quits Docker Desktop.\n\nThe ladder's `tim build gate --phase e2e --holder ibl-…` then:\n- starts Docker\n- rebuilds the stack\n- runs the e2e rungs green\n- stops the stack and deletes the lease\n\nThe next stage runs `tim docker lease status`: lease null, up false.\n\nBy RUN_STACK_RULE that means stackHeld with holder null. confirmStackHeld believes it (null !== RUN_HOLDER).\n\nThe run stops as 'stack-held' and preserves the attempt. Or, where no stage checks, every later e2e gate cold-starts the stack and tears it down.\n\nBetween gates the stack has no lease, so another session can take it. The end-of-run release finds nothing to release.\n\nThe stackDown → recoverDownStack path only fires when a stage reads the lease before any gate has 'healed' it.","severity":"medium","category":"lease-safety","fix":"Make acquireStack return whether the start replaced the holder's own lease (previous?.holder === holder).\n\nHave runE2e give the stack back only when the gate took a lease nobody held before (lease.started && !lease.resumedOwnLease).\n\nAdd a gate test:\n- own UP lease\n- Docker quit\n- e2e runs\n- then assert the lease is still the holder's and the stack is up","verifierNote":"Confirmed on the branch.\n\n- The ladder's e2e gate runs with --holder RUN_HOLDER (increment-build-loop.js:945).\n- takeLease (gate.js:259) calls acquireStack.\n- stackStatus now goes through psStackWithDocker (stack.js:159), which reopens Docker Desktop instead of throwing.\n- The compose files under docker/stack set no `restart:` policy, so the stack comes back with no containers.\n- acquireDecision (stack-lease.js:146) then returns 'start' for the holder's own UP lease when up is false.\n- startUnderLease returns started:true, gate.js:429 calls giveBack, and releaseStack runs stop-stack.sh and removes the lease file.\n\nstack-lease.js and gate.js's lease logic are unchanged in the diff.\n\nBefore the diff, though, the daemon-down case threw inside takeLease ('Can't tell whether the workspace stack is free'). It never reached this path, so the diff is what makes it reachable.\n\nUnit and FIT gate phases' port checks now reopen Docker too. So the ladder's e2e gate is usually the first thing to see the down stack.\n\nrecoverDownStack only runs if a stage reads the lease first. After that, either:\n- a stage sees lease null and stops as stack-held (confirmStackHeld treats a null holder as not this run's)\n- or every later e2e gate cold-starts and releases the stack\n\nMedium is right. It costs lease safety and run continuity, but no wrong code lands green.\n\nSuggested fix: carry 'restarted own lease' from acquireStack and skip giveBack in that case.","also":[]},{"rank":5,"ranks":[5],"id":"backend-fetch-f1","area":"Backend fetch timeout and retry","priority":2,"title":"Two divergent HTTP paths; the CDP path is no longer exercised by any test environment","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend (identical in trade-imports-plants-frontend)","file":"src/server/common/helpers/call-counts/backend-fetch.js","line":21,"what":"connectBoundedDispatcher() returns undefined whenever HTTP_PROXY is set. So fetchOnce (line 58) splits behaviour by environment:\n- Unit, FIT and the local E2E stack send through npm undici 8.11.2's fetch, with an undici FormData copy and a private Agent. They have no HTTP_PROXY, because docker/stack sets none.\n- CDP, which injects HTTP_PROXY, sends through Node's built-in fetch, with Node's FormData, through the global ProxyAgent.\n\nThe comment at lines 17-20 says the fork is needed because built-in fetch 'rejects' npm undici agents. The pre-fix logs show otherwise.\n\ninc-109-ladder-e2e-rerun at 20:13Z, before 19a76c54 at 20:26Z, shows built-in fetch already connecting through /home/node/node_modules/undici/lib/core/connect.js with a 10000ms timeout.\n\nThis is because npm undici's lib/global.js installs its Agent under both:\n- 'undici.globalDispatcher.2'\n- and, wrapped in a Dispatcher1Wrapper, the legacy '.1' symbol\n\nSo the parallel fetch was never needed.\n\nIn CDP the change adds nothing:\n- there is no 2s bound\n- the retry fires only if the socket to the squid proxy itself fails\n\nA backend the proxy cannot reach comes back in one of two ways, and neither is in CONNECT_ERROR_CODES:\n- as a 502/503 response (http target)\n- as RequestAbortedError 'Proxy response (503) !== 200 when HTTP Tunneling' (https target)","scenario":"A future change is made to how bodies or headers are built. For example, another FormData field, a stream body or a Blob subclass.\n\nIt passes unit, FIT and local E2E, because all of them take the undici path.\n\nIt then breaks only in CDP, on the built-in fetch path.\n\nThe reverse has already happened:\n- 19a76c54 sent every upload as text/plain on the undici path.\n- The backend refused it with HttpMediaTypeNotSupportedException.\n- d66b1dce had to patch that in, and only the E2E baseline caught it.","severity":"medium","category":"correctness","fix":"Revert backendFetch to `countBackendCall(); return fetch(url, init)`.\n\nDrop asUndiciFormData/forUndici and test/setup-backend-fetch.js.\n\nIf a local connect bound is still wanted, set it in one place without forking fetch.\n\nFor example, in setup-proxy's no-proxy branch call `setGlobalDispatcher(new Agent({ connect: { timeout } }))` from npm undici. Built-in fetch already honours it through the legacy symbol.\n\nGate it behind a stack-only env var, so CDP and the tests run the same fetch and FormData.","verifierNote":"Confirmed.\n\nbackend-fetch.js:21-26 returns no dispatcher when httpProxy is set. So fetchOnce (line 58) splits calls:\n- CDP calls go through built-in fetch, Node FormData and the global ProxyAgent\n- unit, FIT and the local stack go through npm undici fetch, an undici FormData copy and a private Agent\n\nThe fork was not needed. npm undici's lib/global.js installs its Agent under 'undici.globalDispatcher.2' and also, wrapped in Dispatcher1Wrapper, under the legacy '.1' symbol.\n\nThe pre-fix log inc-109-ladder-e2e-rerun (20:13:49Z) shows built-in fetch failing inside /home/node/node_modules/undici/lib/core/connect.js with 'timeout: 10000ms'.\n\nSo setGlobalDispatcher(new Agent({connect:{timeout}})) in a stack-only branch would have bounded the connect without a second fetch path.\n\nThe 19a76c54 → d66b1dce text/plain upload regression shows this divergence already broke once.\n\nMedium is right: no CDP break today, but nothing exercises the CDP path any more.","also":[]},{"rank":6,"ranks":[6],"id":"backend-fetch-f4","area":"Backend fetch timeout and retry","priority":2,"title":"FIT global timeout raised to fit full-journey set-up repeated per one-page test","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"package.json","line":23,"what":"d1884244 raised two limits because the suite outgrew 900s:\n- --global-timeout from 900000 to 1500000\n- the FIT job timeout-minutes from 20 to 30 (.github/workflows/check-pull-request.yml:115)\n\nThe CI evidence:\n- main run 38217892519 ran 455 tests in 12.7m (762s, already 85% of the old cap)\n- the combined branch run 38219575045 ran 529 tests in 18.0m\n- both had 0 flaky and 0 retried tests, so the raise is not hiding flakes\n\nThe slowdown is more than the extra tests explain:\n- the average test went from 1.67s to 2.04s\n- 20 tests now take 15s or more (1 on main)\n\nEvery one of those is a one-page assertion. Its beforeEach drives sign-in, every answer section, the health certificate upload, submit and the dashboard.\n\nExamples:\n- check-answers.fit.spec.js:876-902 ('submitted notification', 9 tests, added in 40c3b6cc/df59fbde)\n- cancel-amend.fit.spec.js:85-89 (submitNotification then amend)\n- dashboard.fit.spec.js submitted/amending rows","scenario":"Each new assertion about a submitted or amending notification adds about 16s of repeated journey driving.\n\nThe 1500s cap is reached after about 25 more such tests. The next raise follows the same pattern.\n\nMeanwhile real per-test slowness has 420s of headroom to grow into without anyone seeing it.","severity":"medium","category":"test-performance","fix":"For these one-page assertions, use a seeded submitted (or amending) notification for set-up. The house rule allows this for single-page assertions.\n\nKeep the full submit journey in one journey test.\n\nThen set the budget from the measured run (roughly 1.3× runtime) rather than a round 1500s.","verifierNote":"d1884244 raised:\n- --global-timeout from 900000 to 1500000 (package.json:23)\n- the FIT job timeout from 20 to 30 (check-pull-request.yml:115)\n\nThe 'submitted notification' describe in check-answers.fit.spec.js (around line 876) runs the whole journey in beforeEach: sign-in, every section, the health-certificate upload, submit and the dashboard. It uses a 90s per-test timeout.\n\nThe pattern existed on main, but only with 2 tests and no upload.\n\nThe build added several more tests and an upload step. So it multiplied the cost rather than inventing it.\n\nI did not re-check the CI run IDs and timings, but the code shape supports the claim.\n\nThe house rule allows seeding to set up a one-page assertion, so the fix is in policy.","also":[]},{"rank":7,"ranks":[7,9],"id":"animals-fe-shared-f1","area":"Animals frontend shared code","priority":2,"title":"Transit and destination lists now offer subdivision codes that reach GBN-AG as raw country IDs","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"src/server/app/services/countries/index.js","line":121,"what":"destinationCountryOptions() (via countryAndSubdivisionOptions) now offers subdivision codes such as FR-MQ or PT-30 as values.\n\nThe transit country search and the import-reason destination select now take their allow-list from it.\n\nOrigin handles subdivisions in two fields. countryOfOriginSubdivisionCode is mapped by notification-mapper/mapper-a/sections/origin.js to parentCountryCode(code) plus countrySubdivisionCode.\n\nTransit has no such split:\n- mapper-a/sections/transport.js sends transitedCountries through unchanged\n- the backend's SpecifiedConsignment then builds TradeCountry.fromCountryCodes(transport.getTransitedCountries())\n- that wraps each string as CodedValue.of(code)\n- so the GBN-AG TradeCountry ID becomes 'FR-MQ' instead of an ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 code\n\nSam's ruling (2026-10-09, items 26-27) says to offer the subdivisions. But neither inc-006 nor inc-174 looked at the outbound mapping.\n\nThe inc-006 plan only checked that the backend's destinationCountry is a free string.","scenario":"A trader on a road consignment searches 'Martinique', picks 'Martinique (France)' and submits.\n\n- The notification stores transitedCountries ['FR-MQ'].\n- The animals backend outbox puts SpecifiedConsignment.transitCountries [{ID: 'FR-MQ'}] in the GBN-AG message.\n- The downstream consumer rejects it or misreads the country.\n\nThe same territory chosen as the origin is sent correctly, as FR plus subdivision FR-MQ.","severity":"medium","category":"cross-feature","fix":"Settle the contract before release. Either:\n- map transited and destination subdivisions the way origin does, in mapper-a/sections/transport.js and wherever destinationCountry leaves the service. Origin uses the parent country code, with the subdivision carried separately if GBN-AG has a field for it.\n- or confirm with the GBN-AG/IPAFFS owners that subdivision codes are valid there\n\nAdd a mapper test for a subdivision transit code.","verifierNote":"Confirmed on the branch.\n\n- transit-countries/validate.js now builds offeredCodes and offeredCountries from countries.destinationCountryOptions(). That includes subdivision keys such as FR-MQ (countries/index.js countryAndSubdivisionOptions).\n- Before this change transit used originCountries(), which held countries only.\n- mapper-a/sections/transport.js:45 passes transitedCountries through as reader.scalar. The diff leaves that file untouched.\n- In the backend, TradeCountry.fromCountryCodes (outbox/gbnag/TradeCountry.java:21-27) wraps each string as CodedValue.of(code) with a null subdivision.\n- SpecifiedConsignment.java:43 sends that list.\n\nSo a picked subdivision reaches GBN-AG as ID 'FR-MQ'. Origin splits the same code into a parent country plus a subdivision.\n\nNothing in the backend checks the codes.\n\nOn destinationCountry: it goes through direct-fields.js and NotificationCopyMapper only. I found no GBN-AG outbox use, so the release risk is mainly transit.\n\nMedium is right. It is a contract defect that the downstream consumer may reject, and no test covers it.","also":[{"rank":9,"id":"feature-folders-f1","file":"src/server/app/sets/live-animals/journeys/linear/features/transport/transit-countries/validate.js","line":23,"repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","what":"offeredCountries now returns countries.destinationCountryOptions(), which includes subdivisions such as ES-CN.\n\n- Validation accepts these codes, and the controller saves them into transitedCountries.\n- import-reason's destination selects do the same.\n\nIn the animals backend, SpecifiedConsignment builds the transit list with TradeCountry.fromCountryCodes(transport.getTransitedCountries()). That wraps each code unchanged as CodedValue.of(code).\n\nThe origin page maps a subdivision back to its parent (parentCountryCode) for the region-code prefix. But nothing on the transit or destination path maps or splits a subdivision.\n\nThe backlog deliberately asks for the subdivision code to be saved. But no increment checked what the outbound GBN-AG message does with it.","scenario":"A rail consignment adds 'Canary Islands (Spain)' as a transit country, so transitedCountries is ['ES-CN'].\n\nWhen submitted, the GBN-AG payload carries a TradeCountry with code ES-CN where an ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 country code is expected.\n\nThe downstream system may reject the message or mis-route it. The frontend fit tests cannot see this.","fix":"Before merge, confirm with the GBN-AG/PIMS mapping (docs/analysis/gbn-ag-field-lineage.md) whether a subdivision code is valid in the transit and destination country fields.\n\nIf it is not, map it to its parent in the backend outbox mapper, not in the frontend. Send the subdivision separately if the schema has a slot for it.\n\nAdd an outbox test with a subdivision code either way.","verifierNote":"Confirmed.\n\n- transit-countries/validate.js now builds offeredCodes and offeredCountries from countries.destinationCountryOptions().\n- The new unit test 'Should commit a subdivision code and continue' saves ['FR','ES-CN'].\n- In the backend, TradeCountry.fromCountryCodes (outbox/gbnag/TradeCountry.java:21) wraps each code unchanged as new TradeCountry(CodedValue.of(code), null). It leaves the subordinateTradeCountrySubDivision slot it has empty.\n- So ES-CN goes out as a country code.\n\nThe backlog row (arrival-and-transit, 'Save and continue saves its code') asks for this on Sam's 9 Oct ruling. But no row checks the outbound GBN-AG message.\n\nOne part of the claim is overstated. destinationCountry is not mapped into the gbnag outbox at all (no match in outbox/). So the GBN-AG risk is limited to transit countries.\n\nMedium stands because the result is an outbound message to an external system that may reject it, and no test covers it."}]},{"rank":8,"ranks":[8],"id":"ins-frontend-f1","area":"INS frontend","priority":2,"title":"Countries 503 still logs no fetch cause: Boom puts the cause in data, not error.cause","repo":"trade-imports-ins-frontend","file":"src/server/app/services/countries/index.js","line":17,"what":"stackWithCauses follows error.cause.\n\nThe only 5xx Boom this app raises around a fetch is Boom.serverUnavailable('Reference data unavailable', { dataset, cause: err }).\n\n@hapi/boom has no cause option. So the fetch error goes into response.data.cause, and response.cause stays undefined.\n\ncatchAll then logs only the Boom stack.\n\nThe new test builds Boom.boomify(new TypeError('fetch failed', { cause })). This code path never produces that shape.","scenario":"On CDP, reference-data is unreachable (for example the egress proxy or DNS fails).\n\n- GET /address-book/add\n- ensureLoaded throws Boom 503\n- catchAll logs 'Error: Reference data unavailable' and its stack, with no 'Caused by:' line\n\nThe socket or DNS error this commit was meant to expose is still missing from the log.","severity":"medium","category":"correctness","fix":"Use Boom.boomify(err, { statusCode: 503, message: 'Reference data unavailable', data: { dataset: 'countries' } }). This keeps the original error and its cause chain.\n\nOr attach the cause to the Boom (Object.assign(boom, { cause: err })).\n\nAdd a test that drives ensureLoaded with nock replyWithError and checks the logged 'Caused by:' line.","verifierNote":"Confirmed.\n\ncountries/index.js:17 calls Boom.serverUnavailable(message, data). The diff does not touch it, but it is the main 5xx path the new errors.js logging is for.\n\n- @hapi/boom 'serverUnavailable(message, data)' passes data through internals.serverError to options.data (lib/index.js:117-118, 381-383).\n- boom never sets err.cause, so response.cause is undefined.\n- The dashboard controller rethrows Boom errors (controller.js:114-115).\n- So an unreachable reference-data service reaches catchAll as a 503. stackWithCauses prints only the Boom stack, with no 'Caused by:'.\n\nOne correction: the test shape (Boom.boomify of a TypeError with a cause) is not impossible.\n\nIt is exactly what hapi does to an uncaught non-Boom error thrown from a handler. So the new code does work for uncaught throws.\n\nIt does not work for the countries outage, which is the one fetch path this app wraps in Boom itself.\n\nFix as suggested: boomify the original error with statusCode 503, or keep the cause on the Boom.","also":[]},{"rank":10,"ranks":[10],"id":"plants-features-f1","area":"Plants frontend feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"title":"Outstanding-row error links trap a half-done row in a Check your answers loop","repo":"trade-imports-plants-frontend","file":"src/server/app/sets/high-risk-plants/journeys/linear/features/check-answers/view-model/outstanding-rows.js","line":45,"what":"outstandingRowHref sends every row error to rowEntry(row) (the row's FIRST gate-passing page), wrapped in withChange (?change=1).\n\n- The pages post back to their own URL (form has no action).\n- kit.exitTarget turns any ?change=1 save into a redirect to notification-view.\n\nWhen the row is outstanding because a LATER page in it is unanswered, the trader is sent to the first page. That page is already answered.\n\nSaving there returns them to Check your answers with the same error. The page that is actually missing is never reached.","scenario":"Plants-for-planting draft with arrivalStatus = not-yet-arrived and no arrivalDate (or a potatoes draft with commodityType set but no commodity lines).\n\n- Continue on Check your answers gives 'Complete arrival details' (or 'Complete what you are importing').\n- The link opens /arrival-status?change=1 (or /commodity-type?change=1) with the radio already checked.\n- Save and continue: arrival-status always calls nextTarget, which goes back to /notification-view. commodity-type does the same when no lines are removed.\n- The same error shows again, so the trader loops until they find another way in through Overview.\n\nThe fit test only covers potatoes, where arrival-status is out of scope, so it misses this.\n\ncontroller.test.js asserts href `${base}/commodity-type?change=1`, which fixes the defect in place.","severity":"medium","category":"correctness","fix":"Link each row error to the first gate-passing page in the row whose collects are not yet fulfilled. Do not link it to the row's first page.\n\nUse statusOf per page to find it.\n\nOr keep the change context only for single-page rows, and send multi-page rows to the hub row entry without ?change=1.\n\nAdd tests:\n- a plants/wood test for the arrival row (status answered, date missing)\n- a test for a typed commodity row with no lines","verifierNote":"Confirmed on the branch.\n\n- outstanding-rows.js:45-48 is new. It links each row error to rowEntry(row) plus ?change=1.\n- rowEntry (flow/navigation.js:20-23) returns the row's first gate-passing page, whatever has already been answered there.\n- Both arrival-status and commodity-type have a form with no action (`<form method=\"post\" novalidate>`), so the POST keeps ?change=1.\n- kit.exitTarget (kit.js:133-137) then sends the trader to CYA_SLUG.\n- arrival-status/controller.js always ends with nextTarget. commodity-type does the same unless lines were removed.\n- The arrival row is [arrivalStatusPage, arrivalDetailsPage]. The commodities row is [commodityTypePage, commoditiesPage, commodityDetailsPage] (task-rows.js:30,33).\n\nSo a row with its first page answered and a later page missing sends the trader to the answered page. From there they go straight back to Check your answers with the same error.\n\ncontroller.test.js:336-346 seeds potatoes with no lines and asserts href `${base}/commodity-type?change=1`, which is exactly this trap.\n\nThe trader can still get out through Overview or a summary-row Change link. So it is a navigation loop, not data loss.\n\nMedium stands.","also":[]},{"rank":11,"ranks":[11],"id":"animals-backend-f3","area":"Animals backend","priority":1,"title":"No IT covers the path that clears action-required, or a clean submit's event","repo":"trade-imports-animals-backend","file":"src/test/java/uk/gov/defra/trade/imports/animals/integration/NotificationIT.java","line":938,"what":"The existing outbox event ITs (around lines 1340, 1444 and 1488) were re-pointed from SUBMITTED to SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED instead of being given a clean ITAHC.\n\nTheir comments still say 'through SUBMITTED'.\n\nAs a result, no integration test asserts the normal event a correct submit produces: a clean ITAHC giving notificationStatusCode SUBMITTED.\n\nNo IT drives the designed recovery path either:\n- SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED\n- then amend, upload a clean ITAHC, resubmit\n- then SUBMITTED with outstandingItems [] on the stored aggregate, and the NotificationSubmissionAmended event\n\nThat path is unit-tested only, with DocumentService mocked.","scenario":"Suppose a regression makes resubmission ignore documents uploaded during an amendment. For example, it reads documents for the wrong reference, or reads before the upload is persisted.\n\nEvery IT stays green.\n\nTraders are left stuck at 'Submitted action required' after uploading their certificate.","severity":"low","category":"test-coverage","fix":"Add ITs through the real endpoints for both paths:\n- First: submit, amend, givenACleanItahc, submit. Assert status SUBMITTED, outstandingItems empty, and the last event's notificationStatusCode.\n- Second: a clean-ITAHC first submit, asserting the event's notificationStatusCode is SUBMITTED.\n\nFix the stale 'through SUBMITTED' comments in the re-pointed tests.","verifierNote":"Partly overstated but real.\n\nA clean-ITAHC submit IT exists (NotificationIT:867). But it asserts only the aggregate status and outstandingItems.\n\nIt does not check the outbox event's notificationStatusCode. The only IT on that field is line 938, which pins SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED.\n\nNo IT drives the path SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED, then amend, upload a clean ITAHC, resubmit. createAndSubmitNotificationWithFullContent seeds the ITAHC before the first submit.\n\nThe stale comment at line 1341 ('through SUBMITTED') sits above an assertion of SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED, as the reviewer said.\n\nThis is a test gap only, so low.","also":[]},{"rank":12,"ranks":[12],"id":"workspace-tooling-f2","area":"Workspace tooling","priority":1,"title":"Failed-attempt handler's soft reset sweeps up commits that are not the attempt's","repo":"trade-imports-workspace","file":".claude/skills/requirements-pipeline/workflow/increment-build-loop.js","line":1511,"what":"New step 0 of the branch-lifecycle preserve prompt treats every commit in `origin/<branch>..HEAD` as the failed attempt's work:\n- it writes them out as a patch\n- then runs `reset --soft origin/<branch>`\n- and later steps stash the result\n\nThat rests on 'No stage commits: only landing does, and it pushes at once'.\n\nBut the loop has a push-failed outcome that leaves a landed, recorded, unpushed commit on the branch by design ('a resume pushes the recorded commit').\n\nThe branch baseline (branchBaselinePrompt) never checks the ahead count.\n\nA person's own unpushed commit on the theme branch also passes the clean-tree baseline.","scenario":"- Row A lands, its push is rejected because the remote moved, and the run stops push-failed with A's commit SHA recorded.\n- The person relaunches with an explicit id queue, or the derived order puts row B first.\n- B's ladder goes red. preserveAttempt soft-resets A's commit together with B's work into B's failure stash.\n- A's row still records its commit, so A resumes past build.\n- The find-PRs stage sees nothing ahead to push and marks A done.\n\nA's change never reaches the branch. It survives only in logs/<B>-preserve-*.commits.patch.","severity":"low","category":"correctness","fix":"Either:\n- make the baseline refuse to start a row when any configured repo has commits ahead of origin/<branch>. Or push them first, as the find-PRs stage does.\n- or have step 0 reset only commits made after a HEAD SHA the loop records at the start of the row's baseline","verifierNote":"Step 0 (increment-build-loop.js:1510-1517, added in 4d734946) does soft-reset everything in origin/<branch>..HEAD in every configured repo.\n\nThe push-failed outcome (4363-4371) does leave a recorded, unpushed commit.\n\nbranchBaselinePrompt (2848) checks only the branch and clean trees, never the ahead count. So the scenario can happen.\n\nIt needs three things:\n- a push-failed stop\n- a relaunch before the human reconciles the branch (which the code comment expects them to do)\n- a different row's red ladder before A is pushed\n\nA different row comes first only with an explicit id queue or a derived order that puts B first.\n\nNothing is lost outright. The commits go to logs/<B>-preserve-*.commits.patch and the note names their SHAs.\n\nA's row would still show a commit that is not on the branch, though. That is a silent integrity gap.\n\nThe scenario is narrow and the work can be recovered, so low rather than medium.\n\nThe fix the reviewer suggests is sound: refuse at baseline when ahead > 0, or record a per-row HEAD and reset only past it.","also":[]},{"rank":13,"ranks":[13],"id":"workspace-tooling-f3","area":"Workspace tooling","priority":1,"title":"Theme spec check stops the run on gaps that predate the theme, and on prefixes the theme never touched","repo":"trade-imports-workspace","file":".claude/skills/requirements-pipeline/workflow/increment-build-loop.js","line":3253,"what":"themeSpecCheck turns either of these, across the whole prefix, into a 'spec-check-red' stop:\n- any `tim spec gaps --none` noneCount > 0\n- any lint finding\n\nThat includes gaps that predate the theme.\n\nIt also adds every prefix reachable from the repos of theme rows this run did not build:\n- a row with no `repos` counts as touching every configured repo\n- animals-backend maps to both live-animals and germinal-products","scenario":"A theme was built over two launches. Its earlier rows changed only trade-imports-animals-backend for live animals.\n\nWhen the last row lands, the 'repo reach' source adds germinal-products/.\n\nThat prefix has unproven scenarios from before this programme. So the run stops with SPEC CHECK RED.\n\nIt prescribes 'catch-up and cover germinal' for a set the theme never touched.","severity":"low","category":"correctness","fix":"Compare against a per-prefix gaps baseline taken at the run's (or theme's) first baseline. Stop only on new noneScenarios.\n\nFor rows not built in this run, use the prefixes recorded in their landed Spec sync lines instead of repo reach. Those lines are already parsed.","verifierNote":"Confirmed.\n\n- prefixesBySource (3243-3250) adds 'repo reach' for rows this run did not build.\n- SPEC_PREFIXES_BY_REPO_PATH (753-762) maps both animals repos to ['live-animals','germinal-products'].\n- gapsProblem (3209) turns any noneCount > 0 into a problem.\n- themeSpecCheck stops the run with spec-check-red.\n\nA theme built over two launches can therefore stop on germinal-products gaps it never touched. The run then prescribes 'catch-up and cover germinal'.\n\nStopping on gaps that predate the theme, within prefixes the theme did touch, is arguably the intended strict policy. That follows Sam's 'failures are mine to fix' stance.\n\nThe repo-reach over-inclusion is the real defect.\n\nThe effect is a false stop at the end of a theme with a clear message, not wrong code, so low.\n\nThe better fix is to use the recorded landed Spec sync prefixes for rows not built in this run.","also":[]},{"rank":14,"ranks":[14],"id":"workspace-tooling-f5","area":"Workspace tooling","priority":1,"title":"FIT deny rule blocks only test:fit, not test:fit:features or test:fit:journeys","repo":"trade-imports-workspace","file":".claude/skills/requirements-pipeline/workflow/increment-build-loop.js","line":1364,"what":"FIT_ON_STACK_PORT = /\\brun test:fit(?!:)/ is meant to refuse plan checks that serve a frontend on the stack's port.\n\nThe negative lookahead lets any `test:fit:<suffix>` through.\n\nIn animals-frontend and plants-frontend:\n- `test:fit:features` and `test:fit:journeys` call `npm run test:fit` with no PORT\n- playwright.config.js sets reuseExistingServer: false","scenario":"A planner writes `npm --prefix ~/…/trade-imports-plants-frontend run test:fit:features -- tests/x.spec.js` as a section-5 check.\n\n- The deny list accepts it.\n- The webServer can't bind 3003 (the leased stack holds it).\n- The ladder goes red, preserving and stopping a correct increment.","severity":"low","category":"correctness","fix":"Deny every test:fit form except test:fit:ci, e.g. /\\brun test:fit(?!:ci\\b)/, and add a contract test case for test:fit:features.","verifierNote":"Confirmed.\n\nFIT_ON_STACK_PORT = /\\brun test:fit(?!:)/ (line 1364) lets test:fit:features and test:fit:journeys through.\n\n- In both frontends these scripts call `npm run test:fit` with no PORT.\n- plants playwright.config.js uses PORT ?? 3003 with reuseExistingServer: false.\n- So on a leased stack holding that port, the webServer cannot start.\n\nA planner who writes test:fit:features as a section-5 check gets past the deny list. The ladder goes red with nothing broken.\n\nLow, because the effect is a false red stop, which preserves the work.\n\nThe fix /\\brun test:fit(?!:ci\\b)/ plus a contract test is right.","also":[]},{"rank":15,"ranks":[15],"id":"workspace-tooling-f4","area":"Workspace tooling","priority":1,"title":"Read-only stack queries now launch Docker Desktop and can block for 5 minutes","repo":"trade-imports-workspace","file":"tim/src/exec/stack.js","line":159,"what":"Every caller of stackContainers or stackContainerIds now goes through psStackWithDocker. The callers are:\n\n- lease status\n- gate held-port checks\n- writeStackLogs\n- recordLeaseContainers\n\nOn macOS, whenever the daemon is unreachable, that runs `open -a Docker`. It then polls for up to TIM_DOCKER_START_WAIT_MS (default 5 minutes).\n\nSome machines have a docker context of Colima or OrbStack. There, the daemon-unreachable stderr matches, but `open -a Docker` fails.\n\nThe user then sees 'Docker Desktop would not open' instead of the real cause.","scenario":"Docker is deliberately stopped. Someone runs `tim docker lease status` to check who holds the stack.\n\nDocker Desktop opens and the command hangs for up to 5 minutes.\n\nwithStackLogs can stall the same way after a red e2e rung.","severity":"low","category":"correctness","fix":"- Restart Docker only on the start paths (run-stack.sh and acquire).\n- Let status reads report 'Docker not running' as up:false with a reason.\n- Check `docker context show` before assuming Docker Desktop.","verifierNote":"Confirmed.\n\nThe chain stackStatus → stackContainerIds → psStackWithDocker → ensureDockerRunning (docker-daemon.js:81) runs `open -a Docker`. It polls for up to TIM_DOCKER_START_WAIT_MS (5 minutes by default).\n\nSo these all launch Docker Desktop as a side effect of a read:\n\n- `tim docker lease status`\n- the gate's port checks\n- writeStackLogs\n\nThe 5-minute hang happens only when Desktop opens but never answers. A normal start takes well under a minute.\n\nThe Colima and OrbStack case is real but hypothetical on this team, which runs Docker Desktop.\n\nIt is a design wart rather than a breaking bug.\n\nIt also feeds f1, because it makes the stack read heal the daemon silently.\n\nLow.","also":[]},{"rank":16,"ranks":[16],"id":"perf-f2","area":"Performance tests","priority":1,"title":"Missed-dashboard warning is logged on every VU iteration and includes the OIDC callback URL with its auth code","repo":"trade-imports-performance-tests","file":"src/k6/front-door.js","line":64,"what":"openInsDashboard calls console.warn(describeMissedPage(...)) on every failed dashboard check.\n\nThat call is on the hot path of:\n\n- dashboardOnlySession\n- addressBookSession\n- every journey session\n\nEvery other console call in src/k6 and src/suites runs in setup or teardown.\n\ndescribeMissedPage puts the full page.url in the line. The commit's own test shows this is the /auth/sign-in-oidc?code=... callback when sign-in fails.","scenario":"Defra ID or the INS session store degrades during a peak-day or spike run on CDP.\n\nEvery dashboard-opening session writes a warn line holding the authorisation code from the callback URL. That is hundreds a minute.\n\nThe k6 log fills with near-identical lines. They hide the setup and teardown summary lines.\n\nThe codes end up in CDP's log store.\n\nThey are short-lived and come from the stub. But it is a bad pattern to copy across to real identity providers.","severity":"low","category":"security","fix":"- Strip the query string before logging. pathOf in pages.js already does this.\n- Log once per VU, or add to a Counter tagged with status and heading. With the Counter, the summary shows what the missed page was without one line per iteration.","verifierNote":"Confirmed in src/k6/front-door.js:61-65.\n\nconsole.warn(describeMissedPage(...)) runs on every failed dashboard check inside openInsDashboard. That is on the per-session hot path.\n\ndescribeMissedPage puts in the full url, query string included. The commit's own test case shows a /auth/sign-in-oidc?code=... URL.\n\nThe reviewer is slightly overstated in saying every other console call is in setup or teardown. call-counts.js and resilience.js also log at run time.\n\nStill, nothing else logs once per iteration on a failure path.\n\nThe security risk is minimal, because:\n\n- the code reaching the log has already been spent on the failed callback\n- in perf, it comes from the Defra ID stub\n\nThe flooding during a degraded run is real.\n\nFix:\n\n- strip the query with pathOf\n- either log once per VU or count failures with a tagged Counter","also":[]},{"rank":17,"ranks":[17],"id":"perf-f1","area":"Performance tests","priority":1,"title":"k6 live-animals walk still out of step with the real opening run after additional-details; cph-number is no longer a run step","repo":"trade-imports-performance-tests","file":"src/k6/live-animals.js","line":208,"what":"The frontend's RUN_STEPS (animals-frontend src/server/app/sets/live-animals/journeys/linear/flow/run.js) now runs:\n\n- origin\n- commodities\n- import-reason\n- consignment-details\n- identification\n- additional-details\n- port-of-entry\n- transit-countries\n- transporters\n- documents\n- addresses (endsRunWhenComplete)\n- contact\n- notification-view\n\nThis change also removed cph-number from the run. It is reached only from its row on the addresses page.\n\nThe k6 draft fixes only the first six steps. It still walks:\n\n- additional-details\n- documents\n- addresses\n- cph-number\n- port-of-entry\n- transit-countries\n- transporters\n- contact\n\nwalker.reach (src/k6/pages.js) throws away the page a save landed on when its path differs from the next step's. It opens the expected page directly instead.\n\nThe `saved` check only tests status 200, no stale redirect and no redirect loop.\n\nSo every mismatch costs a GET a real user never makes, and nothing fails.\n\nThe new live-animals.test.js claims the order is 'as Design Release 2.1 orders them'. But it pins only names.slice(0, 6), so the wrong tail is locked in, not caught.","scenario":"A single-session live-animals notification during peak-day.\n\n- (1) The additional-details save lands on /port-of-entry. k6 discards it and GETs /documents.\n- (2) The addresses save lands on /consignment/contact/select, or on the hub when nothing is outstanding. k6 GETs /cph-number, a page the run no longer visits.\n- (3) The cph-number save falls through nextInTaskRow back to /addresses. k6 GETs /port-of-entry.\n- (4) The transporters save lands on /documents. k6 GETs /consignment/contact/select.\n\nThat is 4 extra page renders per notification. About 2 of them are new since the opening-run change.\n\nEach is counted in page_requests and run.counter.\n\nreEditUntilTarget then cuts the padding re-edits to keep pagesPerNotification. So the per-endpoint mix drifts from the traffic model:\n\n- documents, cph-number, port-of-entry and contact GETs go up\n- re-edit saves go down\n\nEvery check stays green.","severity":"low","category":"correctness","fix":"- Reorder the draft tail to match RUN_STEPS: additional-details, port-of-entry, transit-countries, transporters, documents, addresses, contact.\n- Model cph-number as a detour inside the addresses step. Open it from the addresses page, save it, land back on addresses, then save addresses.\n- Drop cph-number from its own top-level slot.\n- Change live-animals.test.js to assert the full draft order.\n\nBetter still, make reach record a counter or warn (reuse describeMissedPage). It should do this when, during the opening run, it has to open a page directly instead of landing on it.\n\nThe next frontend reorder then fails loudly.","verifierNote":"Confirmed on the branch.\n\nThe animals-frontend diff to flow/run.js:\n\n- removes cphNumberPage from RUN_STEPS\n- adds endsRunWhenComplete on addresses\n\nThe k6 draft in src/k6/live-animals.js (lines 208-218) still walks documents, addresses, cph-number, port-of-entry, transit-countries, transporters, contact.\n\nwalker.reach in src/k6/pages.js:150 keeps the landed page only when the status is 200 and the path matches. Otherwise it opens the expected path directly. saved() only checks isSaved.\n\nMismatches therefore cost extra GETs and nothing fails.\n\nlive-animals.test.js pins only names.slice(0, 6).\n\nI am downgrading it from medium.\n\nMost of the tail mismatch was already on main, since main's frontend also ran port-of-entry before documents.\n\nThe only new drift is addresses → cph-number. Main's run went from addresses to cph-number, and now it goes to contact. That is about one extra direct GET per notification.\n\nReal users still open cph-number from its row on the addresses page. So it stays a real page in the mix.\n\nThresholds and measurements are unchanged. So this is a small skew in the traffic model, not a broken measurement.\n\nThe suggested fix is sound:\n\n- reorder the tail\n- make cph-number a detour from addresses\n- assert the full order\n- warn or count when reach falls back mid-run","also":[]},{"rank":18,"ranks":[18],"id":"stub-ref-f3","area":"Stub and reference-data","priority":1,"title":"An unrecognised MDM traffic value silently becomes type null and sorts last","repo":"trade-imports-reference-data","file":"src/main/java/uk/gov/defra/trade/imports/portsofentry/PortType.java","line":42,"what":"fromTraffic matches 'Airport', 'Port' and 'Rail' exactly, ignoring case only.\n\nIt does not trim. No log or metric records a miss.\n\nPortOfEntry maps a miss to null. PortListOrder puts null types after rail.\n\nEvery observed value is one of the three. But only CDP-test MDM was captured, not production.\n\nMDM's data is known to be messy:\n\n- duplicates\n- 'Teestort'\n- non-breaking spaces","scenario":"Production MDM returns traffic 'Port ' (trailing space), 'Seaport', 'Sea Port' or 'Airport/Port' for a real port.\n\nreference-data serves it with \"type\":null after Folkestone, below every rail port.\n\nA user scanning the seaports A to Z does not find it.\n\nNothing in the logs shows that MDM's vocabulary has changed.","severity":"low","category":"correctness","fix":"- Strip the traffic value before matching.\n- Log a warning once per unrecognised value (code plus raw traffic) when a PortOfEntry is built. Production drift then shows up.\n- Optionally, capture production or pre-production MDM traffic values before release.","verifierNote":"Confirmed in PortType.fromTraffic (lines 41-48).\n\nIt uses equalsIgnoreCase with no strip(). It logs nothing on a miss.\n\nThe test keepsAPortOfUnknownTypeLast pins a null type that sorts last.\n\nEvery captured value matches one of the three. So the trailing-space or 'Seaport' scenario is hypothetical drift in production MDM, not a current fault.\n\nLow.\n\nThe fix is cheap: strip the value and log a warning on a miss.","also":[]},{"rank":19,"ranks":[19],"id":"stub-ref-f2","area":"Stub and reference-data","priority":1,"title":"The stub's blocks filter is presented as MDM's behaviour, but the evidence also fits another explanation","repo":"trade-imports-stub","file":"src/main/java/uk/gov/defra/trade/imports/stubs/mdm/countries/CountriesController.java","line":28,"what":"The Javadoc says blocks 'keeps the records that carry that block whatever includeCountry says, as MDM does'.\n\ncarriesBlock looks only at the country-level blocks[].name.\n\nThe accepted spec (stub-tidy-up-spec.md:48-49) admits a second explanation. GB may come back because of its GB-NIR subdivision bloc.\n\nGB's own 'GBNAG_SPS_EX' block name is also an inference. The jq program renamed every masked '******_EX' token.\n\n35 other block entries have no name at all (masked). So filtering on any block other than GBNAG_SPS_EX returns [].\n\nMatching is exact and case-sensitive.\n\nReal MDM's behaviour is unknown for:\n\n- an unknown block\n- a comma-separated list\n- different case\n\nBut a test now pins [] for an unknown block.","scenario":"A future consumer asks for blocks=EU, or for a block that a country reaches only through a subdivision bloc.\n\nThe stub returns [] or a different set from real MDM.\n\nLocal and CDP-dev tests then pass against behaviour that production will not show.\n\nNothing breaks today:\n\n- reference-data is the only caller\n- it keeps only includeCountry=true\n- it drops GB","severity":"low","category":"stub-fidelity","fix":"- Change the Javadoc and the 'High fidelity' doc row to say what was observed (one SPS capture). They should also say that unnamed blocks are not filterable.\n- Confirm the selection rule before relying on it. Confirm it with the MDM team, or with one more capture of a block reached only through a subdivision.","verifierNote":"Confirmed.\n\nThe CountriesController Javadoc (lines 28-31) says 'as MDM does'.\n\nstub-tidy-up-spec.md:48-49 itself gives a second explanation: GB could come back through its GB-NIR bloc.\n\nLine 79 says the masked block names were left out. It says GBNAG_SPS_EX was used for the proven entries.\n\ncarriesBlock is an exact, case-sensitive match on the country-level blocks[].name only.\n\nNothing breaks today. The only caller is reference-data, and it asks for GBNAG_SPS_EX alone (the fixture has 32 matches).\n\nThe risk is the Javadoc and the fidelity row overclaiming, so low is right.","also":[]},{"rank":20,"ranks":[20],"id":"stub-ref-f4","area":"Stub and reference-data","priority":1,"title":"New reference-data controller test injects mocks into the controller and mocks an internal service","repo":"trade-imports-reference-data","file":"src/test/java/uk/gov/defra/trade/imports/portsofentry/PortsOfEntryControllerTest.java","line":17,"what":"The test uses @ExtendWith(MockitoExtension) with @InjectMocks on the controller.\n\nIt mocks MdmService. That is an in-process collaborator, not the HTTP boundary.\n\ndocs/best-practices/java/testing/unit.md says:\n\n- never use @InjectMocks\n- test controllers with @WebMvcTest\n\nSam's rule is to mock only at the network boundary.\n\nThese edge cases are proved only on Java objects, never through Feign decoding and Jackson serialisation:\n\n- null or unknown traffic giving \"type\":null on the wire\n- non-breaking spaces\n- ties broken by code\n- case-insensitive matching\n\nThe WireMock IT covers six well-formed ports.","scenario":"Someone later adds @JsonInclude(NON_NULL), or changes PortType's @JsonValue or deserialisation.\n\nUnknown-type ports then lose their type key, or the frontend gets a different wire shape.\n\nEvery unit test stays green, because none of them serialises.\n\nThe IT has no unknown-traffic or missing-traffic port to catch it.","severity":"low","category":"test-behaviour","fix":"- Add a port with unknown traffic, one with no traffic and one with a non-breaking space to src/test/resources/integration/mdm-poe-response.json.\n- Assert the order and the \"type\":null wire shape in PortsOfEntryControllerIT.\n- Test PortListOrder and PortType directly as pure units, with no mocks.\n- Drop the @InjectMocks controller test.","verifierNote":"Confirmed.\n\nPortsOfEntryControllerTest uses @ExtendWith(MockitoExtension), @Mock MdmService and @InjectMocks on the controller.\n\ndocs/best-practices/java/testing/unit.md:42 says 'Never use @InjectMocks'.\n\nThe test mocks an in-process service, not the network boundary.\n\nEvery edge case (unknown or null traffic, NBSP, tie on code, case) is asserted only on Java objects. None of them goes through Feign decoding or the JSON wire shape (\"type\":null).\n\nThe breakage scenario is a later regression slipping through, not a present bug. So low.","also":[]},{"rank":21,"ranks":[21],"id":"stub-ref-f1","area":"Stub and reference-data","priority":1,"title":"The jq programs that rebuild the stub fixtures, and the stub doc, are not committed","repo":"trade-imports-workspace","file":"workareas/shared/design-release-2-1/mdm/regenerate/countries.jq","line":1,"what":"The 256 KB countries fixture and the ports fixture can only be rebuilt from the raw masked captures. The programs that do this are workareas/shared/design-release-2-1/mdm/regenerate/{countries,ports}.jq.\n\n`git status` shows that folder as untracked (??).\n\nThe docs/repos/trade-imports-stub.md edit is uncommitted (' M'). That edit:\n\n- names those programs\n- raises the stub's fidelity from 'Shape only' to 'High'\n\nThe plan said 'the orchestrator commits the workspace', but nothing was committed.\n\nThe stub repo's own README and the commit say nothing about where the fixtures came from.","scenario":"Someone resets or cleans the workspace checkout, or reviews only the stub PR.\n\nThe jq programs are lost. With them goes the only record of:\n\n- which masked fields were left out\n- which were recovered from cdp-test captures (effectiveAlias, the GBNAG_SPS_EX block names, GB MID's spaces, the townCity values)\n\nMDM changes, and nobody can regenerate the fixture the same way.\n\nThe next person hand-edits 10k lines or recaptures without the masking rules.","severity":"low","category":"provenance","fix":"- Commit regenerate/*.jq and the stub doc edit on the workspace branch of the same name.\n- Add one line to the stub README (or to the commit history the PR carries). It should point to the captures and the regenerate programs.","verifierNote":"Confirmed. `git status` shows:\n\n- workareas/shared/design-release-2-1/mdm/regenerate/ as untracked\n- docs/repos/trade-imports-stub.md as modified but not staged\n\nNothing breaks at runtime.\n\nThe fixtures are committed in the stub repo and the raw captures are tracked. So regenerating them would be harder but still possible.\n\nThis is a handover task (commit the programs and the doc on the workspace branch), not a code defect. So I have lowered it to low.","also":[]},{"rank":22,"ranks":[22],"id":"tests-repo-f3","area":"Tests repo","priority":1,"title":"Negative check on the review runs before the review page has loaded","repo":"trade-imports-ins-tests","file":"tests/animals/e2e/features/additional-details-scope.spec.ts","line":87,"what":"After `reviewAndSubmitButton.click()`, the test immediately runs `expect(getByText('Includes unweaned animals')).toHaveCount(0)`.\n\nIt does not wait for the review heading.\n\nThe Overview never shows that text. So the assertion passes at once while the browser is still on the Overview.\n\nAs written, it can never fail.","scenario":"The commodity change stops clearing the unweaned answer. So the review still shows the 'Includes unweaned animals: Yes' row.\n\nThe check at line 87 runs before navigation finishes and passes on the Overview.\n\nOnly the later re-open check (radios unchecked) would catch the regression. It would catch it only if the review row and the stored answer go wrong together.","severity":"low","category":"test-weakened","fix":"- Wait for the review first: `await expect(animalsPages.notificationView.heading).toBeVisible()` plus a URL check for /notification-view$.\n- Then run the toHaveCount(0) check.\n\nBetter still, check that the 'Additional details' card's terms are exactly the expected list.","verifierNote":"Confirmed at additional-details-scope.spec.ts:86-87.\n\nThe reviewAndSubmitButton.click() is followed straight away by toHaveCount(0). There is no wait for the review heading or URL.\n\nThe Overview does not carry that text, so the check can pass before navigation finishes.\n\nThe impact is smaller than claimed. If the stored answer were not cleared, the re-open check at :96 would catch it (Yes not checked after cattle is chosen again).\n\nOnly one regression would go unnoticed: the review still shows the row while the stored answer is cleared.\n\nFix as suggested: wait for the review URL and heading first.","also":[]},{"rank":23,"ranks":[23],"id":"tests-repo-f5","area":"Tests repo","priority":1,"title":"Reference-list answer tests still accept any next page after the merge","repo":"trade-imports-ins-tests","file":"tests/animals/e2e/features/reference-lists/destination-country-list.spec.ts","line":48,"what":"Commit f510245 loosened the post-save check to `url.pathname !== importReason url`, 'whatever page comes next'.\n\nThat let the reference-lists theme pass both with and without journey-foundation underneath.\n\nOn the combined branch, the destination is settled. A reason opened from its Overview task saves back to the Overview.\n\nThe loose predicate is still there at:\n\n- destination-country-list.spec.ts:48 and :69\n- port-of-exit-list.spec.ts:52\n\nThe test at :69 also no longer checks that there is no error summary.","scenario":"A regression makes Save and continue on Main import reason go to an error page or the wrong task.\n\nThe URL changes, so the predicate passes.\n\nThe test then opens the Overview itself and checks only the stored value. So the wrong navigation goes unnoticed in these specs.","severity":"low","category":"test-weakened","fix":"All themes are now merged. So change these to `await expect(animalsPages.overview.heading).toBeVisible()` plus the 'There is a problem' toHaveCount(0) check.\n\nThat matches what reason-purpose-scope.spec.ts already asserts for the same save.","verifierNote":"Confirmed at destination-country-list.spec.ts:48 and :69 (a new file on the branch).\n\nThe predicate only requires the pathname to differ from the import-reason page.\n\nA server error re-rendered at the same POST path would still fail it. So the reviewer's 'error page' example is weaker than stated.\n\nA misroute to the wrong task would pass, though. And :69 has no 'There is a problem' check.\n\nThe tests' main intent (the answer is stored and re-selected) is still covered. reason-purpose-scope.spec covers the navigation.\n\nTightening to overview.heading plus the error-summary check is cheap now that the themes are merged.","also":[]},{"rank":24,"ranks":[24],"id":"tests-repo-f6","area":"Tests repo","priority":1,"title":"Verdict on 65b6afc: superseded, not needed, safe to delete","repo":"trade-imports-ins-tests","file":"tests/animals/e2e/features/promoted-documents.spec.ts","line":39,"what":"65b6afc exists only on backup/inc-107-leaked-commit.\n\nNo other branch contains it, and none of its content is on the combined branch.\n\nIt adds:\n\n- (1) the 'reads each saved document back on the review in its own Document card' test, using references PWA/PWB and a new 250-byte PNG\n- (2) a new tracked fixture resources/file-upload/safe-file-250b.png plus a `.gitignore` negation `!resources/file-upload/*.png`\n- (3) a rename of `PW-TXT-` to `PWTXT` in the unsupported-file-type test\n\nItems 1 and 2 are not needed. Commit 02dd6bc (inc-107, on the combined branch) landed the same test with alphanumeric references PWDOCA/PWDOCB and the existing safeFile100kbJpg fixture.\n\nItem 3 is not needed either. The frontend's file-type check (documents/form/errors.js fileErrors) rejects the .txt before the backend's `@Pattern(^[a-zA-Z0-9]*$)` on documentReference is reached.\n\nSo the hyphenated reference does not change which error appears.","scenario":"Cherry-picking 65b6afc onto the combined branch would:\n\n- add a second copy of the document-card test under the same title, which conflicts with 02dd6bc\n- widen .gitignore to track PNGs under resources/file-upload/ for no current user","severity":"low","category":"stray-commit","fix":"Delete backup/inc-107-leaked-commit. Nothing needs carrying over.\n\nOptionally, change the unsupported-file-type test (promoted-documents.spec.ts:91). Make it assert the file-type message rather than the generic 'There is a problem'.\n\nIt then cannot pass because of a different error.","verifierNote":"This is a housekeeping verdict, not a code defect.\n\nChecked:\n\n- `git branch -a --contains 65b6afc` shows only backup/inc-107-leaked-commit.\n- promoted-documents.spec.ts:39 on the combined branch already has the document-card test with PWDOCA/PWDOCB.\n- The unsupported-file test still uses PW-TXT- (:88).\n- As claimed, the animals frontend's documentAddErrors runs fileErrors (FILE_TYPE_MESSAGE for a .txt) locally. So the hyphenated reference never reaches the backend's @Pattern.\n\nDeleting the backup branch is safe.\n\nTightening :91 to the file-type message is a good optional improvement.","also":[]},{"rank":25,"ranks":[25],"id":"backend-fetch-f3","area":"Backend fetch timeout and retry","priority":2,"title":"ETIMEDOUT in the retry set is not guaranteed to mean the request was never sent","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend (identical in trade-imports-plants-frontend)","file":"src/server/common/helpers/call-counts/backend-fetch.js","line":14,"what":"The retry runs for every method, including:\n\n- POST /notifications (create)\n- /{id}/copy\n- /{id}/submit\n- /amend\n- /soft-delete\n- /notifications/{id}/document-uploads\n- /document-uploads/{id}/file\n\nIt fires if any cause in the chain has UND_ERR_CONNECT_TIMEOUT, ECONNREFUSED or ETIMEDOUT.\n\nThe first two can only happen before any request bytes are sent. That includes during the TLS handshake for an https target. So retrying on them is safe.\n\nETIMEDOUT can also be raised on an established keep-alive socket. It comes from a kernel keepalive or retransmission failure after the request has been written.\n\nThe logs only show ETIMEDOUT nested under UND_ERR_CONNECT_TIMEOUT (AggregateError from internalConnectMultiple). So it adds no coverage.","scenario":"POST /notifications/{id}/submit is written on a reused keep-alive socket. The backend commits it.\n\nThe path then drops before the response arrives. The socket errors with ETIMEDOUT.\n\nbackendFetch sends the submit again.\n\nThe second call gets a 409 or state error. On create or copy, it gets a duplicate notification.\n\nThis is unlikely within undici's 300s headersTimeout, but possible.\n\nNothing guards against it.","severity":"low","category":"idempotency","fix":"If the retry survives:\n\n- limit CONNECT_ERROR_CODES to UND_ERR_CONNECT_TIMEOUT and ECONNREFUSED\n- preferably retry only GET/PUT/DELETE, or require an idempotency key for POST","verifierNote":"ETIMEDOUT is in CONNECT_ERROR_CODES at line 14.\n\nisConnectError walks the whole cause chain for every method, including POST submit, create and copy.\n\nundici's h1 client passes a socket error on an established socket straight to in-flight requests as the raw error. The exception is ECONNRESET at message end.\n\nSo a kernel ETIMEDOUT after the request was written would be retried.\n\nThe unit test at backend-fetch.test.js:110 shows the intent is not to resend once the connection has opened. ETIMEDOUT contradicts that intent.\n\nIt is rare and needs a mid-flight path drop.\n\nLow.","also":[]},{"rank":26,"ranks":[26],"id":"backend-fetch-f2","area":"Backend fetch timeout and retry","priority":2,"title":"Unit tests cannot see what is actually sent; the real-connection tests pass with the feature removed","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend (identical in trade-imports-plants-frontend)","file":"test/setup-backend-fetch.js","line":9,"what":"A global setupFiles vi.mock('undici') replaces undici.fetch with a shim. Before calling the mocked global fetch, the shim:\n\n- strips `dispatcher`\n- converts undici FormData back to Node FormData\n\nThat mocks a library and rewrites its arguments, rather than mocking at the network boundary.\n\nSo every unit test of a backend call sees a request shape production never sends.\n\nThe conversion code in backend-fetch.js (forUndici/asUndiciFormData) is not observable to them.\n\nIn backend-fetch.real.test.js:\n\n- 'tries a refused connection once more' (line 104) only asserts the final ECONNREFUSED. It gets that with or without a retry.\n- 'reaches the backend through its connect-bounded agent' (line 18) passes with the agent removed.\n\nNo test exercises the 2s bound.","scenario":"Delete the retry branch (lines 74-78) or the `dispatcher` from forUndici. Every real-connection test still passes.\n\nA FormData conversion bug like the one 19a76c54 shipped passes the whole unit suite.\n\nThis is because the shim reconverts the body before any assertion sees it.","severity":"low","category":"test-weakened","fix":"If the code is removed (f1), delete the shim and the real test.\n\nIf any of it is kept, test only against a real local server:\n\n- count 'connection' events to prove exactly two attempts\n- to prove the time bound, use a listener that accepts the TCP connection but never answers TLS, or MockAgent\n\nDo not mock undici globally in setupFiles.","verifierNote":"Partly real.\n\nThe setupFiles vi.mock('undici') in test/setup-backend-fetch.js:9-26:\n\n- strips the dispatcher\n- converts undici FormData back to Node FormData\n\nUnit tests therefore cannot see the agent or the FormData conversion. And it mocks a library rather than the network boundary.\n\nIn the real-connection tests:\n\n- test 1 passes with no agent\n- test 4 (line 104) asserts only the final ECONNREFUSED\n\nSo neither proves a second attempt.\n\nNothing tests the 2s bound.\n\nThe scenario overstates one point. Deleting the retry branch at lines 74-78 WOULD fail backend-fetch.test.js:73-89, which drives the retry through the shim.\n\nThe real multipart test at lines 37-77 now partly closes the FormData gap.\n\nWhat remains is an unproven timeout and agent, plus a library-level mock. So low rather than medium.","also":[]},{"rank":27,"ranks":[27],"id":"backend-fetch-f5","area":"Backend fetch timeout and retry","priority":2,"title":"Explanatory comments added against the house rule","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend (identical in trade-imports-plants-frontend)","file":"src/server/common/helpers/call-counts/backend-fetch.js","line":6,"what":"These comments explain the rationale:\n\n- lines 6-7, 10, 17-20 and 43-44\n- the 6-line header in test/setup-backend-fetch.js\n\nThey explain:\n\n- the Docker Desktop forwarder\n- why there are two fetches\n- why FormData is copied\n\nThe house rule is no explanatory comments. Rationale belongs in docs and git history.\n\nThe comment at 17-20 is also factually misleading (see f1).","scenario":"A reader trusts lines 17-20: 'Node's built-in fetch cannot take an agent from the undici package'.\n\nThey keep the fork.\n\nBut the logs show built-in fetch already running on npm undici's Agent.","severity":"low","category":"stray-comment","fix":"Removed along with the code under f1.\n\nIf anything is kept, move the rationale into the commit message or docs/repos/<repo>.md.","verifierNote":"These are rationale comments, which break the no-explanatory-comments rule:\n\n- lines 6-7, 10, 17-20 and 43-44\n- the 6-line header in test/setup-backend-fetch.js\n\nOne nuance: lines 17-20 may be literally true for passing an npm undici Agent as a per-call 'dispatcher' to built-in fetch. That is because of the v1/v2 handler API split.\n\nThey still mislead, since the global-dispatcher route works. The 20:13Z log proves it.","also":[]},{"rank":28,"ranks":[28,31],"id":"animals-fe-shared-f3","area":"Animals frontend shared code","priority":2,"title":"Amend 'Save and return' skips required checks on import reason only","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"src/server/app/shared/kit.js","line":151,"what":"skipsRequiredChecks is documented as the rule for:\n\n- 'Save and return to overview'\n- while amending, 'Save and return' to the review\n\nOnly the import-reason controller calls it.\n\nThese five pages pass allowMissing: kit.isHubExit(request):\n\n- origin\n- port of entry\n- transit countries\n- transporters\n- additional details\n\nThe new save-actions macro gives every amend page the same three controls. But on those five pages, amend 'Save and return' to the review still runs the full required checks.\n\nThe inc-022 plan narrowed this on purpose ('another row's decision'). But no backlog row picks it up.\n\nSo the controls look the same and behave differently from page to page.","scenario":"A trader amending a submitted notification opens Arrival details from a Change link.\n\nThey clear the arrival time and press 'Save and return'. The page refuses with the required-time error.\n\nOn Main import reason, the same action with a blank port of exit saves and returns to the review.","severity":"low","category":"cross-feature","fix":"Decide the rule once. Do one of these:\n\n- Switch every soft-save call site to kit.skipsRequiredChecks(request, journey). Add one E2E amend case per page.\n- Record in the backlog or a ruling that amend 'Save and return' keeps required checks everywhere except import reason. Narrow the helper's doc comment to match.","verifierNote":"Confirmed.\n\nkit.js:149-151 documents skipsRequiredChecks as covering amend 'Save and return' to the review. But only import-reason/controller.js:101 calls it.\n\nThese pages pass allowMissing: kit.isHubExit(request):\n\n- origin (88)\n- port-of-entry (102)\n- transit-countries (153)\n- transporters (144)\n- additional-details (80)\n\nSo an amend save from a Change link still runs the required checks on those pages. The controls are shared, so the same action behaves differently from page to page.\n\nThere is no data-integrity risk:\n\n- the stricter pages fail closed\n- submit stays gated\n\nIt is a UX inconsistency between the doc comment and the call sites, so low.","also":[{"rank":31,"id":"feature-folders-f2","file":"src/server/app/sets/live-animals/journeys/linear/features/import-reason/controller.js","line":101,"repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","what":"kit.skipsRequiredChecks is documented as true for:\n\n- 'Save and return to overview'\n- 'Save and return' to the review while amending\n\nOnly import-reason uses it.\n\nThese pages pass allowMissing: kit.isHubExit(request):\n\n- origin (origin/controller.js:88)\n- port-of-entry\n- transit-countries\n- transporters\n- additional-details\n\nSo the same amend 'Save and return' button runs the required checks there. The same control behaves differently from page to page.","scenario":"While amending, the trader opens Main import reason from a Change link (?change=1). They leave the reason blank and press 'Save and return'. It saves and goes back to the amend review.\n\nThey then open Arrival details from a Change link. They leave the required means of transport unanswered and press 'Save and return'.\n\nThe page refuses with 'Select a means of transport to the port of entry'.","fix":"Decide which behaviour inc-004 and c-211 intend for amend 'Save and return'.\n\nThen use the same predicate on every page that renders amendFormActions. Either:\n\n- skipsRequiredChecks everywhere\n- or isHubExit on import-reason too\n\nAdd one controller test per page for the amend return path.","verifierNote":"Confirmed in code.\n\nThe amend 'Save and return' button posts to ?change=1 with no exit=hub (shared/amend-form-actions.js, save-actions.njk).\n\nkit.skipsRequiredChecks is used only in import-reason/controller.js:101.\n\nThese pages use isHubExit, so on them the same button runs the required checks:\n\n- origin (88)\n- port-of-entry (102)\n- transit-countries (153)\n- transporters (144)\n- additional-details (80)\n\nHowever, the difference was decided on purpose. inc-022 plan decision 5 says 'use it in the import reason controller only… the other pages' amend behaviour is another row's decision'.\n\nThe behaviour differs from page to page as the reviewer describes. But nothing is lost or corrupted, and it is a planned deferral rather than a slip.\n\nI lowered it to low and suggest tracking it as the missing follow-up row."}]},{"rank":29,"ranks":[29],"id":"ins-frontend-f2","area":"INS frontend","priority":2,"title":"Controller catch blocks still log fetch failures without their causes","repo":"trade-imports-ins-frontend","file":"src/server/common/helpers/errors.js","line":21,"what":"The commit says it fixes 'A 500 from a failed backend call logged only TypeError: fetch failed'.\n\nBut the dashboard and every address-book controller catch non-Boom errors themselves:\n\n- they log them with logger.error({ err }, ...)\n- they return a 500 view\n- those errors never reach catchAll\n\nThe logger uses @elastic/ecs-pino-format. Its error formatter writes only err.stack as error.stack_trace. This was checked in node_modules/@elastic/ecs-helpers/lib/error-formatters.js.\n\nSo the causes are still dropped there.","scenario":"- ins-backend times out.\n- GET /\n- listNotifications rejects with TypeError('fetch failed', { cause: ConnectTimeoutError }).\n- dashboard/controller.js:117 logs { err }.\n- The ECS line holds only 'TypeError: fetch failed', with no host or connect-timeout detail.\n\nThis is the same diagnostic gap the commit claims to close.","severity":"low","category":"correctness","fix":"Add the causes at the logger instead of in catchAll.\n\nFor example, add a pino err serializer or formatter in logger-options.js. It would write stackWithCauses(err) into error.stack_trace.\n\nThen catchAll can log { err: response } like every other call site. That also follows the pino-logging best practice of always logging { err }.","verifierNote":"Factually right.\n\n- Every controller catch block logs logger.error({ err }, ...) (dashboard/controller.js:117, add/edit/delete/list/view controllers).\n- @elastic/ecs-helpers formatError writes only err.stack into error.stack_trace.\n- logger-options.js adds no err serializer.\n- So causes are dropped there, and those errors never reach catchAll.\n\nThe commit message ('A 500 from a failed backend call logged only TypeError: fetch failed') therefore claims more than it fixes.\n\nSeverity lowered to low:\n\n- the catch blocks predate the diff\n- nothing regressed\n- the gap only costs diagnostics\n\nThe dashboard path renders a recoverable-error view rather than a 500.\n\nMoving cause handling into a pino err serializer is the right fix. It would also cover f1's catchAll path.","also":[]},{"rank":30,"ranks":[30],"id":"ins-frontend-f5","area":"INS frontend","priority":2,"title":"Fixture test asserts static data, and the exact count breaks on every recapture","repo":"trade-imports-ins-frontend","file":"src/server/app/services/_capture/fixtures.test.js","line":19,"what":"fixtures.test.js checks the contents of a checked-in JSON file:\n\n- 249 entries\n- AF first\n- Aland Islands second\n- JE present\n\nNo app behaviour is tested.\n\nThe test only fails when someone runs npm run capture:reference-data and MDM has changed. That is the refresh the script exists for.","scenario":"- MDM adds or renames a country.\n- A developer recaptures.\n- toHaveLength(249) or the order assertions fail.\n- The test gets 'fixed' by editing the numbers.\n\nThe test protects nothing.","severity":"low","category":"test-weakened","fix":"Delete the count and order assertions.\n\nIf a guard is wanted, keep one shape check: an array of { code, name } with no GB.\n\nThe behaviour is already covered by run-mode.test.js and the address-book tests.","verifierNote":"Confirmed.\n\nfixtures.test.js imports a checked-in JSON file and asserts:\n\n- its length (249)\n- its first two entries\n- that JE is present\n\nIt tests no app behaviour and fails only on a legitimate recapture. That conflicts with Sam's no-coverage-padding and test-behaviour rules.\n\nThe 'no GB' assertion has some value as a guard. address-countries.js adds GB itself, so a GB in the fixture would duplicate it.\n\nKeep that check plus a { code, name } shape check. Drop the count and order assertions.","also":[]},{"rank":32,"ranks":[32],"id":"feature-folders-f4","area":"Animals frontend feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"title":"maxlength=5 on the region code suffix silently cuts off a code typed with its prefix","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"src/server/app/sets/live-animals/journeys/linear/features/origin/template.njk","line":35,"what":"The suffix box now has maxlength 5.\n\nServer-side, suffixOf in origin/validate.js deliberately accepts a value typed with the country prefix ('FR-75').\n\nWith maxlength 5, the browser truncates that input before the server sees it. So the forgiving path can never receive a full prefixed code.\n\nGOV.UK guidance also advises against maxlength on text inputs, because users are not told their input was cut.","scenario":"France is the country. The trader pastes 'FR-12345' from the health certificate.\n\n- The browser keeps 'FR-12'.\n- suffixOf strips 'FR-'.\n- The page stores 'FR-12'.\n- The review shows a wrong region code with no error.","severity":"low","category":"accessibility","fix":"Drop the maxlength attribute. Rely on the existing server-side REGION_CODE_SUFFIX_MAX_LENGTH check, which reports the error.\n\nIf the design insists on maxlength:\n\n- remove the prefix-forgiving branch, so behaviour is predictable\n- update the fit test that asserts the attribute","verifierNote":"Confirmed.\n\n- The diff adds attributes: { maxlength: '5' } to origin/template.njk.\n- origin/validate.js:59-65 suffixOf still strips a typed 'FR-' prefix ('Also forgives a user who typed the prefix themselves').\n\nThe server-side REGION_CODE_SUFFIX_MAX_LENGTH = 5 check can never fire on a pasted 'FR-12345'. The browser has already cut it to 'FR-12'.\n\nThat value passes as region code FR-12 with no error.\n\nThe maxlength was requested in the origin-page backlog (row note at line 229). So this is a conflict in the spec as well as in the code.","also":[]},{"rank":33,"ranks":[33],"id":"plants-features-f2","area":"Plants frontend feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"title":"Welsh view header shows English month names","repo":"trade-imports-plants-frontend","file":"src/server/app/sets/high-risk-plants/journeys/linear/features/check-answers/controller.js","line":84,"what":"longDateText always formats with the 'en-GB' locale.\n\nBut the new copy.cy viewHeader.dateCreated/dateSubmitted wrap it in Welsh.\n\nSo the Welsh page mixes Welsh text with an English date.","scenario":"A draft viewed with the Welsh locale shows 'Dyddiad creu: 22 July 2026' instead of '22 Gorffennaf 2026'.\n\nA submitted view does the same with 'Dyddiad cyflwyno'.","severity":"low","category":"correctness","fix":"Format with the request's locale, or use a shared localised long-date helper.\n\nFor the locale, use 'cy' or 'cy-GB' when Welsh is active. Intl supports Welsh month names.\n\nAlso add a cy assertion to the existing fake-timer test.","verifierNote":"Real.\n\n- longDateText (controller.js:83-89) hardcodes 'en-GB'.\n- The new copy.cy.js:18-19 wraps it in 'Dyddiad creu:' / 'Dyddiad cyflwyno:'.\n- So the Welsh page shows an English month name.\n\nconfirmation/controller.js:25 and declaration/controller.js:29 already format with 'en-GB' in the same way. So the build copied an existing convention rather than adding a new kind of defect.\n\nA fix should be one shared localised date helper, not a change to this file alone.","also":[]},{"rank":34,"ranks":[34],"id":"feature-folders-f3","area":"Animals frontend feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"title":"Draft review page title contradicts inc-111's acceptance criterion","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"src/server/app/sets/live-animals/journeys/linear/features/check-answers/controller.js","line":148,"what":"headed is true for DRAFT, so pageTitle becomes the journey reference.\n\nInc-111 says 'the journey review of a draft or an amendment keeps Review your notification - Import notification service - GOV.UK' (c-006).\n\nInc-187 later changed the draft's h1 to the reference. But it says nothing about the <title>.\n\nThe fit spec 'a draft's review is titled by its reference' pins the changed title. So a live acceptance criterion is now contradicted, and a test locks that contradiction in.","scenario":"A trader opens a draft's review from the hub.\n\nThe browser tab and the screen-reader page title read '<reference> - Import notification service - GOV.UK'. While the notification is incomplete, 'Error: ' comes in front.\n\nThey do not read 'Review your notification - …' as the inc-111 criterion requires.","severity":"low","category":"correctness","fix":"Get a ruling on which wins.\n\n- If inc-111 stands: set pageTitle to copy.title for DRAFT, keep the reference as the h1, and update the fit spec.\n- If inc-187 supersedes it: update the inc-111 criterion and the openspec scenario so they agree.","verifierNote":"Confirmed.\n\ncheck-answers/controller.js sets:\n\n- headed = readOnly || status === DRAFT\n- title = headed ? journey.journeyId : reviewTitle(journey)\n\nIt passes that title as pageTitle.\n\nThe overview-and-review backlog.json:996 (inc-111) still says the review of a draft 'keeps Review your notification - Import notification service - GOV.UK'.\n\nThe draft-header row (backlog.json:1701-1711) changes only the h1 and says nothing about <title>.\n\nThe criteria contradict each other, and the code follows one of them.","also":[]},{"rank":35,"ranks":[35],"id":"feature-folders-f5","area":"Animals frontend feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"title":"species-errors imports a sibling feature's controller and repeats the obligation rule","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"src/server/app/sets/live-animals/journeys/linear/features/check-answers/view-model/species-errors.js","line":5,"what":"The new check-answers view-model module imports scopedFields from commodities/animal-identification/animal-identification.controller.js.\n\nThat reaches into another feature's controller rather than its page.js descriptor.\n\nIt also restates the obligation's multi-species identifier rule in the view model:\n\n- lines.length <= 1 plus 'any unit carries any identifier'\n- this mirrors unitRecord.requires.floorAppliesToParent(moreThanOne(...)) in obligations/sections/commodities/identifiers.js\n\nIf the obligation changes, the error summary drifts from what actually blocks submission.","scenario":"The identifier floor is later changed. For example, it counts species rather than commodity lines, or requires an identifier on every unit.\n\nThe review keeps naming 'Enter at least 1 animal identifier for <species>' by the old rule. Or it names nothing while Continue is refused.\n\nSeparately, a refactor of the animal-identification controller breaks check-answers.","severity":"low","category":"cross-feature","fix":"Get the per-line verdict from the evaluator or scope instead of re-deriving it. It is the same per-instance verdict the bridge's invariants compute.\n\nMove scopedFields to services/commodities, or to a non-controller module. Then neither identifier-columns nor species-errors imports a controller.","verifierNote":"Confirmed.\n\nThe new file check-answers/view-model/species-errors.js:5 imports scopedFields from commodities/animal-identification/animal-identification.controller.js. That reaches into another feature's controller.\n\nidentifier-columns.js already did the same on main.\n\nspecies-errors.js also re-derives the multi-species floor itself ('lines.length <= SINGLE_SPECIES' plus 'any unit carries any identifier').\n\nIts own JSDoc says this is 'the same test the unit record's floor applies' (obligations/sections/commodities/identifiers.js:113 moreThanOne).\n\nThe rule is now kept in two places that can drift apart. This is a design risk with no current wrong output, so low.","also":[]},{"rank":36,"ranks":[36],"id":"feature-folders-f6","area":"Animals frontend feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"title":"Hub wraps every task group in a named section, adding many region landmarks","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"src/server/app/sets/live-animals/journeys/linear/features/hub/template.njk","line":32,"what":"The overview now wraps each of these in a <section> with an accessible name:\n\n- the totals block\n- each totals tile (aria-label)\n- every task-list group\n\nEach one becomes a region landmark. So the page goes from no regions to about nine nested ones.\n\nThat clutters landmark navigation and is not a GOV.UK task list pattern.","scenario":"A screen-reader user opens the landmarks list on the Overview.\n\nThey find these, all nested inside main:\n\n- 'Animals summary'\n- 'Packages summary'\n- the totals region\n- the task list region\n- one region per section group\n\nPreviously the headings gave them the structure.","severity":"low","category":"accessibility","fix":"Keep the headings and remove the section wrappers (or use plain divs).\n\nLeave at most one labelled region, if the design really needs one.\n\nCheck with the accessibility lead before adding landmarks the GOV.UK task list pattern does not use.","verifierNote":"Partly mandated.\n\nbacklog.json:118 (overview-and-review) requires the two tiles to be regions named 'Animals summary' and 'Packages summary'. So those two sections are acceptance criteria.\n\nNo criterion requires the extra wrappers, each with aria-labelledby:\n\n- the totals section\n- the task-list section\n- one section per group\n\nEach becomes a named region landmark.\n\nThe landmark noise the reviewer describes does happen, but it is a minor accessibility concern.\n\nThe fix should keep the two required tile regions.","also":[]},{"rank":37,"ranks":[37],"id":"feature-folders-f7","area":"Animals frontend feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"title":"Explanatory and migration comments added to source and specs","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"src/server/app/sets/live-animals/journeys/linear/features/transport/transit-countries/transit-countries.controller.js","line":38,"what":"The build added comments that explain behaviour or design history, against the house rule.\n\nExamples:\n\n- '// Asked of rail and road consignments only…' (transit-countries.controller.js:38)\n- '// The customs document code a live-animals import declaration quotes (CHED-A), fixed.' (view-header.js:16)\n- the {# A submitted notification is a record… A draft ends with Continue… #} block at the end of check-answers/template.njk\n- the 'Design release 2.1 lays the review out…' and 'Design release 2.1 reads the countries…' comments in check-answers.fit.spec.js\n- '// The error summary lists errors by question…' (commodities/consignment-details/fields.js)\n- '// A radio cannot be un-chosen…' (import-reason/validate.js)","scenario":"The next change to these pages leaves the 'Design release 2.1' prose stating something the code no longer does.\n\nThe prose is a migration narrative. It belongs in docs/openspec, not the source.","severity":"low","category":"stray-comment","fix":"Delete them.\n\nWhere the rationale matters, it already lives in the backlog and openspec. So rely on the names (transitApplies, CUSTOMS_DOCUMENT_CODE) and the tests.","verifierNote":"Confirmed for the cited example.\n\nThe diff adds this comment above transitApplies in transit-countries.controller.js: '// Asked of rail and road consignments only. Out of scope, the page has nothing to ask, so a load or a submit goes to the overview instead.'\n\nIt also rewrites the existing countryItems explanatory comment instead of removing it.\n\nThis breaks the house no-explanatory-comments rule. It is a hygiene issue only, so low.","also":[]},{"rank":38,"ranks":[38],"id":"plants-features-f3","area":"Plants frontend feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"title":"Confirmation now imports from the declaration feature folder","repo":"trade-imports-plants-frontend","file":"src/server/app/sets/high-risk-plants/journeys/linear/features/confirmation/controller.js","line":11,"what":"The build added `import { declarationPage } from '../declaration/page.js'`. It lets confirmation redirect a non-submitted journey to the declaration page.\n\nThat makes the confirmation feature depend directly on another feature folder.\n\nThis breaks the rule that feature folders are independent.","scenario":"Renaming or moving the declaration feature, or changing its page descriptor, now breaks confirmation's import.\n\nThe two folders can no longer be changed or deleted independently.","severity":"low","category":"cross-feature","fix":"Resolve the redirect through the flow layer, rather than importing another feature's page.js.\n\nFor example:\n\n- the review section's page order in flow/flow.js\n- or a slug constant in shared kit, like CYA_SLUG/CANCEL_AMEND_SLUG","verifierNote":"The diff adds `import { declarationPage } from '../declaration/page.js'` at confirmation/controller.js:11, replacing kit.CYA_SLUG.\n\nThat is a new cross-feature dependency against the house rule.\n\nCross-feature imports already exist, so the rule is already bent in places:\n\n- check-answers imports origin/controller.js and review/lateness.js\n- dashboard imports commodity-type/page.js\n\nBut this one is new.\n\nThe fix suggested (a slug constant in kit, like CYA_SLUG and CANCEL_AMEND_SLUG) fits. Low.","also":[]},{"rank":39,"ranks":[39],"id":"plants-features-f4","area":"Plants frontend feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"title":"New ruling-ID and explanatory comments in check-answers controller","repo":"trade-imports-plants-frontend","file":"src/server/app/sets/high-risk-plants/journeys/linear/features/check-answers/controller.js","line":150,"what":"The build added comments that cite ruling IDs or explain the code:\n\n- '// c-201: an amendment opens with no errors; c-136: Continue refused shows them.'\n- '// A UTC instant shown as the Europe/London civil date.'\n- a rewritten '// Ruled c-030: ...'\n\ncommodity-type/controller.js:84 gains a comment that states something the code never checks: 'A blank type only reaches here on Save and return to overview with no type ever saved'.\n\nThe house rules say no explanatory or migration comments. The reasoning belongs in docs and git history.","scenario":"Once ruling IDs c-201/c-136 are renumbered or superseded, the comment points readers at the wrong decision.\n\nThe commodity-type comment will go stale silently if the radios ever become clearable.","severity":"low","category":"stray-comment","fix":"Remove these comments.\n\nName the branch instead. holdsSilently already says what it does.\n\nKeep the c-xxx references in the openspec/plan, not in source.","verifierNote":"The diff confirms these lines are new:\n\n- '// c-201: an amendment opens with no errors; c-136: ...' (controller.js:150)\n- '// A UTC instant shown as the Europe/London civil date.' (line 82)\n- the rewritten '// Ruled c-030' comment (lines 180-181, which existed before but was edited)\n- the commodity-type comment (lines 84-85)\n\nThese are the explanatory and ruling-reference comments the house rules ban.\n\nOne correction: the commodity-type comment is accurate today. validate refuses a blank type unless allowMissing (a hub exit) is set.\n\nIt is a rule breach, not a false statement. Low.","also":[]},{"rank":40,"ranks":[40],"id":"found-in-capture-f1","area":"Found while capturing screenshots (pre-existing on main)","priority":2,"title":"A document reference with a hyphen gives the trader an error page instead of a validation message","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"src/server/app/sets/live-animals/journeys/linear/features/documents","line":1,"what":"The animals backend accepts only letters and digits in a document reference (@Pattern ^[a-zA-Z0-9]*$ on documentReference).\n\nBut the animals frontend has no matching rule.\n\nThe backend's 400 surfaces as a 500 from the frontend's accompanying-documents POST. The page says 'The file could not be uploaded. Try again.'\n\nThis is on main too, so the build did not introduce it.\n\nThe engineering review's tests-repo verifier flagged the same gap.","scenario":"A trader uploads an ITAHC with reference VHC-2026-0001 (or UK-123).\n\nThe POST to /live-animals/notifications/{id}/accompanying-documents returns 500.\n\nThe trader sees a generic upload failure, with no hint that the hyphen is the problem.\n\nReproduced on both main (published images) and the combined branch on 12 October.","severity":"medium","category":"correctness","fix":"Validate the reference in the frontend with the backend's rule and a specific error message.\n\nOr widen the backend rule, if hyphens are legitimate in real certificate numbers. Ask the business.\n\nMap a backend 400 to a page error rather than a 500.\n\nAdd a fit test for the hyphenated case.","verifierNote":"Reproduced directly by the screenshot harness on both branches (screens/before/hyphen-check.json).\n\nNot run through the adversarial verifier.","also":[]}],"killed":[{"id":"tests-repo-f2","area":"Tests repo","title":"INS dashboard test locks in the raw status enum shown to users","severity":"medium","repo":"trade-imports-ins-tests","file":"tests/ins/e2e/features/notification-dashboard-navigation.spec.ts","line":36,"refutation":"The raw enum in the INS table cell is on main, and the build did not change it.\n\n- The raw enum is at features/dashboard/controller.js:54.\n- The ins-frontend fit spec on main already asserts raw 'SUBMITTED' cells.\n- Sam's ruling ruling-sam-2026-10-09-pm.md section (j), point 52, blocks the INS dashboard rows until its requirements firm up and are re-distilled.\n- So the raw-enum table is the accepted interim display.\n\nThe new toContainText('SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED') proves the aggregated read model caught up to the new status. That is a real behaviour worth checking.\n\nA future change to a readable label would update this assertion alongside the fit spec. That is normal test maintenance, not a defect.\n\nThe finding's wider point is that the INS dashboard shows raw enums. That is a known, ruled gap, and this diff did not introduce it."},{"id":"tests-repo-f4","area":"Tests repo","title":"notificationView.heading now matches any review or view page","severity":"low","repo":"trade-imports-ins-tests","file":"page-objects/animals/notification-view-page.ts","line":13,"refutation":"The widened heading follows the design, and the cited scenario is refuted.\n\nThe design gives a draft's review and a submitted view the same H1 (the reference). So a heading-only locator cannot tell them apart.\n\nOther checks sit right beside the heading checks:\n\n- In addresses-submit-freeze.spec.ts, the heading check at :65 after amendNotification is followed at once by statusTag toHaveText('Amend') at :66.\n- The same holds at :126/:128.\n- A misroute to the read-only submitted view would fail there ('Submitted action required').\n- change-from-cya pairs the heading check with toHaveURL, as the reviewer says.\n\nNo spec was shown to rely on the heading alone to tell states apart. So this is a theoretical proxy risk, not a demonstrated weakness."},{"id":"animals-fe-shared-f2","area":"Animals frontend shared code","title":"The error handler logs a plain string, so the err serialiser never runs","severity":"low","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"src/server/common/helpers/errors.js","line":50,"refutation":"The main claim is not a regression from this diff.\n\nOn main the line was already request.logger.error(response?.stack). That also logs a plain string with no { err } object. So the ECS error.* fields were already missing before the build.\n\nThe change only adds the cause stacks to a message that was already a string. That is an improvement on main.\n\nThe infinite-loop part needs a cause chain that loops back on itself.\n\n- Neither undici's 'fetch failed' wrapping nor Boom produces one.\n- The reviewer gives no real source.\n\nSwitching to { err: response } is a fair hardening suggestion. But no failure scenario holds for code this diff introduced."},{"id":"plants-shared-f1","area":"Plants frontend shared code","title":"stackWithCauses loops forever on a cyclic error cause","severity":"low","repo":"trade-imports-plants-frontend","file":"src/server/common/helpers/errors.js","line":18,"refutation":"The loop does have no cycle guard, but the review gives no way to make it spin.\n\nThe loop is in src/server/common/helpers/errors.js:18-27.\n\nI grepped every place plants src sets a cause:\n\n- get-oidc-config-with-retry.js:20\n- services/countries/index.js:27\n- services/ports/index.js:26\n\nEach wraps a fresh `new Error(..., { cause: err })` one level deep. Nothing assigns `.cause` back up the chain.\n\nundici's 'fetch failed' errors also have acyclic causes.\n\nThe 'retry wrapper' in the scenario does not exist in this codebase.\n\nIt is a cheap hardening: keep a Set of seen causes. backend-fetch.js:30-35 walks causes the same unguarded way. But it is not a defect the diff makes reachable."},{"id":"plants-shared-f2","area":"Plants frontend shared code","title":"Amend 'Save and return' keeps required checks in plants but skips them in animals","severity":"low","repo":"trade-imports-plants-frontend","file":"src/server/app/shared/kit.js","line":126,"refutation":"The facts are right. But this is the plants increment working as scoped, not a shared-code defect.\n\nWhat the plants code does:\n\n- Plants kit.js has only isHubExit (line 126).\n- arrival-details/controller.js:192 and the other plants pages pass `allowMissing: kit.isHubExit(request)`.\n- Their templates use amendFormActions.\n- So on plants, an amend 'Save and return' (?change=1) with a required answer blank is refused.\n\nWhy that is as scoped:\n\n- journey-foundation inc-003 (plans/inc-003.md) scopes soft save to 'Save and return to overview' only.\n- Its AC5 for plants proves exactly that.\n\nHow animals compares:\n\n- Animals' skipsRequiredChecks came from a separate per-page increment (fa115bd6, inc-022, commodities-and-reason theme).\n- It is used only on import-reason.\n- Every other animals page also refuses a blank on amend 'Save and return'.\n- So the gap is within animals as well as between the two frontends. It is not a plants regression.\n\nIt is worth one line to Sam as an open product question. The prototype treats the review as a soft-save action.\n\nBut it is not a defect in the changed shared code."},{"id":"ins-frontend-f3","area":"INS frontend","title":"stackWithCauses loops forever on a cause chain that cycles","severity":"low","repo":"trade-imports-ins-frontend","file":"src/server/common/helpers/errors.js","line":24,"refutation":"The missing cycle guard is real in principle, but I found nothing that can produce a cyclic cause.\n\nThe only cause producers are:\n\n- undici fetch errors (a linear chain)\n- get-oidc-config-with-retry.js\n- countries/index.js, which puts its cause in data, so the loop never sees it\n\nNo retry or wrapping code here assigns err.cause back up the chain.\n\nIt is a hardening suggestion, not a demonstrable defect. Adding a Set guard is cheap if someone is already changing the function."},{"id":"ins-frontend-f4","area":"INS frontend","title":"New status shown to users as the raw enum 'SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED'","severity":"low","repo":"trade-imports-ins-frontend","file":"src/server/app/features/dashboard/view-model/list.js","line":112,"refutation":"list.js:112 is not touched by the diff, and every status already shows as its raw code.\n\ninc-113's plan decides this deliberately:\n\n- Decision 12: 'The status cell keeps printing the raw code, as it does for every status today. The dashboard template and copy belong to another theme'.\n- Its out-of-scope list repeats it.\n\nThe fit test asserting 'SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED' matches the existing DRAFT/AMEND/SUBMITTED assertions.\n\nThis is a real, existing GDS gap for the theme that owns dashboard copy. It is not a defect this build introduced."}],"templates":{"headline":"Nothing from the templates merge loses work from either side.\n\nNo template-mode code leaks into a real notification.\n\nThe most serious problem is in the tests:\n\n- Three templates specs compare Andrew's shared draft and template lists before and after an action.\n- So they will fail at random when the 16 parallel workers all sign in as him.\n\nNext come two weaknesses in the build tooling:\n\n- a result file does not say which gate run wrote it\n- a gate that finds the run's own stack down can tear down the run's lease\n\nAfter those come:\n\n- the stale '4. Consignment parties' heading on the template review\n- a mismatch in which templates specs run on CDP\n- three assertions that cannot fail\n- three missing deployment settings: the INS form-action origin, the plants Templates link and the animals link\n\nThe rest are low-risk:\n\n- a GET that creates a draft\n- concurrency and paging gaps in the templates backend\n- stale help text\n- thin tests\n- extra comments","areas":[{"title":"The templates merge resolutions","priority":1,"summary":"I checked the three conflicted merges against both parents:\n\n- animals frontend 74d79f1f\n- INS frontend 340e956\n- tests 17fa81c\n\nI also checked the backend and plants merges, which went through cleanly.\n\nNothing was lost from either side.\n\nShared save-actions macro:\n\n- It combines the template change, amend and hub-exit endings correctly.\n- Template change wins, then amend, then the default.\n- Only the template change ending has no hub exit or formaction.\n- Every page that passed amend before the merge now also passes templateChange by name.\n- The pickers, CPH number and transporter-add pages correctly pass neither.\n\nReview controller split:\n\n- The notification review is byte-for-byte the combined side's.\n- The template review keeps all of templates' behaviour: its title, Save template, no errors, Back to the task list, and no Delete or Cancel amend.\n\nchangeReturnPath:\n\n- Wiring this into exitTarget and the addresses Back link keeps the amend rule.\n- Every page still saves through nextTarget.\n- So a change to a saved template returns to its page, and the \"updated\" banner still shows.\n\nCommodity save and Back link:\n\n- These keep templates' AC.\n- HEAD behaviour is unchanged outside template mode.\n\nReview partial:\n\n- _review-sections.njk, rebuilt on the combined card markup, renders the same cards on the template review and the saved-template page.\n\nTests repo:\n\n- The page-object clean-up, the journeyStripTag rename and the renamed spec strings did not weaken any absence check.\n- summaryCard now matches the exact title.\n- 'Document 1' and 'Identification details' are names the combined branch really renders.\n\nINS frontend:\n\n- Removing the pageTitle override is a no-op.\n\nThere is one real defect from the merge:\n\n- The template review and saved-template page headings were frozen in copy.\n- So the combined branch's section rename did not reach them.\n\nThere is one weakened unit test.\n\nThere is one low risk in the merged surface outside the conflict hunks: a GET that creates a draft.","survived":3,"refuted":0},{"title":"Animals backend: templates (#106)","priority":1,"summary":"Animals backend templates package (#106, 9 commits merged cleanly into the combined branch).\n\nPer-user scoping holds:\n\n- Every read and write goes through findByIdAndOwnerId or findAllByOwnerId.\n- Another user's template answers 404.\n\nNo path writes a template to the outbox, to SNS or to PIMS.\n\nIt uses its own notification_templates collection, with no Mongo event listeners or change streams.\n\nSix findings, none critical.\n\n1. Medium: the updates are read-modify-write with a version the client never sends.\n\n- A double-clicked Save returns a 409 even though the template was saved.\n- Two tabs silently overwrite each other's answers.\n\n2. Medium: the ownership tests are thin.\n\n- The cross-user ITs cover GET and save only.\n- The service unit test mocks the repository.\n\n3. Low to medium: the identity model is a single trusted User-Id header.\n\n- organisationId is recorded but never used.\n\n4. Low: POST /{id}/save is an action path, against the noun-endpoint rule.\n\n5. Low: GET /templates is unbounded.\n\n- It has no paging and returns every template's full answers.\n\n6. Low: there is dead \"legacy row\" handling and wording for rows that cannot exist.\n\n- A null-status row also bypasses the in-progress check.\n\nNot reported: delete and discard, because they are unbuilt rows inc-164 and inc-165.","survived":5,"refuted":1},{"title":"Workspace tooling changed on laptop 2","priority":1,"summary":"The five laptop-2 commits make long gates work, mostly correctly:\n\n- gate-wait waits in slices\n- results are written atomically\n- the parallelRepos worker pool is right\n- stackAfterLocal holds the lease and stack refresh until layer one finishes\n\nThree real risks remain, all in waiting on a long gate.\n\n1. A result file carries nothing that says which gate run wrote it.\n\n- Two gates for the same holder are not stopped from running at once.\n- A gate the agent gave up on keeps running.\n- Its result can land in the next gate's file and be read as that gate's verdict, possibly green on untested code.\n\n2. gate-wait cannot tell a crashed gate from a slow one.\n\n- A gate killed by memory pressure or a tool kill costs up to about 3h10m of waiting before anyone calls it red.\n\n3. These commits do not fix the lease teardown found this morning.\n\n- In that teardown, a gate that finds the run's stack down restarts it under the run's own holder, then releases it at the end.\n- stackAfterLocal makes the window for it bigger.\n\nAlso lower:\n\n- 664beddd stopped writing (or clearing) a result when argument checks or workspace lookup fail.\n- The help, plain-text report and docs still say repos run at the same time and the stack gets ready during layer one.\n\nTest quality is mostly in line with tim/CLAUDE.md (input and output, no spies).\n\nBut nothing tests:\n\n- a dead or overlapping gate\n- a cap between 1 and N\n- the refresh path with a reused lease","survived":5,"refuted":1},{"title":"Tests repo: templates specs","priority":1,"summary":"The templates merge in the tests repo seeds no journeys.\n\ntemplate-parties creates address-book entries through the API, but only as picker set-up. That is the same pattern 10 other specs use.\n\nThe merge retargeted absence checks on the template review, template page and task list.\n\nIt kept those checks able to fail, with one exception: the 'Document 1' card check can never fail.\n\nReview these hardest.\n\n1. Three tests are built to flake.\n\n- All 16 workers sign in as Andrew, and tests run in parallel.\n- These tests need Andrew's draft list and template count to stay the same during the test.\n\n2. Two assertions always pass:\n\n- the one claiming the region code is required\n- the one saying a saved template never shows on the dashboard\n\n3. Most templates specs cross between services and are not tagged @compose, so they run on CDP.\n\n- The three cross-service navigation specs are @compose-only and do not.\n\nI ran no E2E (the stack is in use).\n\nSo these findings come from reading the specs, page objects and merged frontend source.","survived":7,"refuted":1},{"title":"Frontend shared code changed by templates","priority":2,"summary":"I reviewed the shared code the templates merge changed in the animals, INS and plants frontends.\n\nTemplate mode cannot leak into a real notification:\n\n- The template wrapper (AsyncLocalStorage) runs only inside the generated /templates handlers.\n- The records port, path builders, journeyStrip, save-actions and base() only switch inside that wrapper.\n- The notification entry guard only matches /notifications/ paths.\n- A template id never joins the known-journeys cookie.\n- The template records port is scoped to the owner. It refuses create, list, finalise, amend, copy and softDelete.\n- Review submit in template mode short-circuits to saveTemplate.\n- Use template creates its notification outside template mode.\n\nI found 7 real problems.\n\n1. (High) The INS \"Add a new address\" round trip from a template's party picker strands the user on a 404 and loses the address.\n\n2. (Medium) A new INS CSP form-action setting defaults to localhost and is missing from the cdp-app-config checkout.\n\n- On CDP, the type question's hand-over can be blocked when it has to bounce through Defra ID sign-in.\n\n3. (Medium) The plants Templates nav link defaults to http://localhost:3000 and is not set in the cdp-app-config checkout.\n\n4. (Medium) A GET on /start creates a draft in animals and plants.\n\n- That goes against the \"every state change is a form post\" ruling.\n- Prefetch or cross-site links create stray drafts.\n\n5. (Low) Choosing Germinal products in template creation silently drops the user back on Manage templates.\n\n6. (Low) Lighthouse never audits a template-mode page.\n\n7. (Low) Template saves drop the concurrency token, so the last save wins without warning.\n\nNote: the cdp-app-config checkout used for findings 2 and 3 was last committed on 28 Sep.\n\nCheck the live repo before acting on those two.","survived":5,"refuted":2},{"title":"Templates feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"summary":"I sampled three areas:\n\n- the animals frontend templates feature folder\n- the check-answers template-review changes\n- the INS notification-type feature\n\nI found no blockers and no seeded journey tests.\n\n- The FIT specs drive the real UI.\n- The INS controller test mocks only at the network boundary with nock.\n\nThere are six findings, and none is high severity.\n\nTwo are medium.\n\n1. The Manage templates card is hand-built. govuk's flex title wrapper puts the category beside the title instead of above it.\n\n2. The unit tests spy on internal modules (templates service, startJourney, beginOpeningRun). One test builds its expected value by calling the function it is testing.\n\nFour are low:\n\n- new cross-feature imports\n- the saved banner can still be lost if building the cards fails\n- new explanatory comments\n- germinal templates, which can be created today but whose Use template button silently goes back to the list (a known deferral)","survived":4,"refuted":2}],"findings":[{"id":"tests-templates-F1","title":"Before-and-after comparisons on the shared default user flake under parallel workers","repo":"trade-imports-ins-tests","file":"tests/animals/e2e/features/templates/create-template.spec.ts","line":83,"what":"Three tests snapshot Andrew's state, act, then expect it unchanged:\n\n- create-template.spec.ts line 83 checks the Manage templates results line still reads the same.\n- create-template.spec.ts lines 84 and 190 check the draft references on page 1 of the dashboard still match.\n- change-template.spec.ts line 114 makes the same draft-list check.\n\nshared-config sets fullyParallel: true.\n\nauth-state signs every worker in as defaultUser (users.andrew).\n\nSo while these tests run, every other worker's saved template lands in the same lists. So does every live-animals journey spec's new draft.","scenario":"Worker 3 is in create-template's 'leaving before the name page is posted adds nothing' test. It is between reading the 'before' count and the Cancel.\n\nAt that moment:\n\n- worker 7 runs save-template\n- worker 9 runs any journey spec that creates a GBN-AG draft\n\nThe results line moves from 'Show 1-41 of 41' to 'Show 1-42 of 42'. The newest draft heads the dashboard's page 1.\n\nBoth assertions fail even though the feature works.\n\nThe one retry (retries: 1) often hits the same race. So the run goes red or gets labelled flaky.","severity":"high","category":"flake","fix":"Run these tests as an identity no other spec writes to, starting cold. Use:\n\n- browser.newContext({ storageState: COLD_START })\n- manageTemplates.open(true, { userId: <dedicated user> })\n\nThis is what manage-templates.spec does for Sarah.\n\nAlternatively, assert on things this test owns:\n\n- no request to /live-animals/start or /notifications (already captured at line 191)\n- no draft whose origin/internal reference carries this test's unique marker\n\nDo not loosen the equality.","verifierNote":"Confirmed on the branch.\n\n- shared-config.ts sets fullyParallel: true and retries: 1.\n- Sign-in with no options falls back to defaultUser = users.andrew (config/users.ts:23, used by base-page and sign-in-page).\n- create-template.spec.ts:72-84 reads Andrew's results line and page-1 draft references. It runs a multi-page cross-service flow, then asserts exact equality.\n- Lines 177-190 do the same.\n\nIn that window, another worker changes the results line or the page-1 draft list if it:\n\n- saves a template as Andrew (save-template, template-review, view-template, change-template, use-template)\n- or starts a live-animals draft as Andrew\n\ndashboard.draftReferences (added by the merge) reads page 1 only.\n\nAll of this code is new in the merge. Nothing isolates the identity.\n\nThe same file's requestsToAnimals capture at 179-194 already proves the 'nothing created' intent without shared state.","area":"Tests repo: templates specs","priority":1,"ranks":[1],"also":[],"rank":1},{"id":"workspace-tooling-laptop2-GATE-1","title":"A result file does not say which gate run wrote it, so a leftover gate's result is read as the current one","repo":"trade-imports-workspace","file":"tim/src/commands/build/index.js","line":328,"what":"The gate clears gate-result-<phase>.json once, when it starts. It writes the file when it ends.\n\ngate-wait returns whatever file appears at that path.\n\nNothing ties a result to the run that is being waited on:\n\n- no pid\n- no start-time token\n- no lock on the logs folder\n\nStages that repeat reuse one folder:\n\n- the ladder always writes to logs/<id>-ladder\n- fix rounds write to logs/<id>-fix\n- recoverDownStack reruns a stage into the same folder\n\nGATE_WAIT_RULE tells the agent to report \"the gate did not finish\" as red after 20 waits. It never tells the agent to stop the gate, so that gate keeps running.","scenario":"With parallelRepos 1 over five repos, the ladder gate runs past the 600s call plus 20 × 540s waits. That is about 3h10m.\n\nWhat happens next:\n\n- The agent reports it red as \"did not finish\" and the loop moves to fix.\n- The fix stage's unit and FIT gate runs beside the leftover ladder gate. That is two gates at once, which the parallelRepos cap exists to stop.\n- Its FIT rungs go red with \"Port N is in use\" from the leftover gate's FIT server.\n- The next ladder clears gate-result-all.json and starts.\n- The leftover gate then finishes and renames its envelope into that same path.\n- The new ladder's gate-wait returns it straight away.\n\nThat result came from the code as it was before the fix. If it is green, the loop treats code it never tested as green.\n\nBoth gates also share the run's holder. So both refresh or rebuild the same stack services at once.","severity":"medium","category":"correctness","fix":"Give each gate run an identity:\n\n- When it clears the result, write gate-running-<phase>.json with its pid and a nonce.\n- Put the nonce in the result envelope.\n- Have gate-wait return only a result whose nonce matches the marker that was there when the wait began.\n\nAlso do one of these:\n\n- make tim build gate refuse to start while another gate's marker in the same folder names a live pid\n- or take a machine-wide gate lock\n\nThis is needed because the parallelRepos cap only applies within one process.\n\nChange GATE_WAIT_RULE so a gate that runs out of waits is stopped (by pid, from the marker), not left running.","verifierNote":"Holds on the branch.\n\n- gate-result.js waitForGateResult (lines 55-67) returns any envelope at the path.\n- index.js:328 clears it once at start and :338 writes it at the end. There is no run token, pid or lock.\n- increment-build-loop.js:947-960 gives a fixed path per logs folder (gateResultFile).\n- increment-build-loop.js also has GATE_WAIT_RULE report 'the gate did not finish' after MAX_GATE_WAITS=20 without stopping the gate.\n- gates.json now sets parallelRepos 1.\n- The loop's own comment at :950 says a one-repo-at-a-time gate over five repos runs well past 1.5h.\n\nThat makes a gate outrunning about 3h10m plausible.\n\nA later gate in the same folder can then get the leftover gate's envelope. (The ladder reruns into logs/<id>-ladder.)\n\nSeverity is below high. It needs:\n\n- a gate that runs past the full wait budget\n- the backgrounded Bash process to outlive the stage agent that started it, which I could not show for certain","area":"Workspace tooling changed on laptop 2","priority":1,"ranks":[2],"also":[],"rank":2},{"id":"merge-resolution-TPL-MERGE-1","title":"Template review and saved-template page keep '4. Consignment parties', contrary to inc-156's AC, because the headings are hard-coded copies","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"src/server/app/sets/live-animals/journeys/linear/features/check-answers/view-model/template-review.js","line":22,"what":"`asTemplateReview` replaces every section heading with `copy.template.sections[section.anchor]`. That is a separate list of five headings in check-answers copy.en.js:167-173 and copy.cy.js:146-152.\n\nIt does not renumber the review's own headings.\n\nThe combined branch renamed the review's section to '5. Consignment addresses' (inc-101). But the template copy still says '4. Consignment parties'. So the rename never reached template mode.\n\nWhat the backlog says:\n\n- inc-156's AC says the template review shows '4. Consignment addresses' and '5. Contact address'.\n- inc-156's notes say the heading follows inc-101 once it lands.\n- inc-158 says the saved-template page shows 'the review's numbered sections without the documents section, renumbered'.\n\nThe merge log raised this as Question 1 and kept the templates text. But the backlog already answers it.\n\nThese tests all pin the stale wording, so they hide the gap:\n\n- the unit test ('Should show the five sections…')\n- the fit TEMPLATE_SECTIONS\n- the e2e SECTION_CAPTIONS in template-review.spec.ts and view-template.spec.ts\n\nThis is also the drift that ruling claim 5 warns against. The next review heading change will miss template mode again.","scenario":"Create a template and press Review template. Or open a saved template's page.\n\n- Section 4 reads '4. Consignment parties'.\n- On the review of the notification started from it, the same section reads '5. Consignment addresses'.\n- The AC and the design say '4. Consignment addresses'.\n\nThe Welsh pages show the same drift ('4. Partïon y llwyth').","severity":"medium","category":"correctness","fix":"Work the template heading out from the review's own `section.heading`:\n\n- drop the leading 'N. '\n- renumber by position after leaving out the documents section\n\nThen delete `copy.template.sections` in English and Welsh.\n\nUpdate these to '4. Consignment addresses':\n\n- the unit test\n- the check-answers and template-page fit TEMPLATE_SECTIONS\n- the e2e template-review.spec.ts and view-template.spec.ts SECTION_CAPTIONS\n\nLeave the template task list, inc-155 and template-task-list.spec.ts on '4. Consignment parties'. That list follows the hub's group captions, which still say 'Consignment parties'.","verifierNote":"I confirmed this one.\n\nOn the branch:\n\n- template-review.js:22 replaces each section heading with copy.template.sections[anchor].\n- copy/copy.en.js:171 still says '4. Consignment parties'.\n- The review's own heading in copy.en.js:67 is now '5. Consignment addresses' (inc-101 has landed on combined).\n\nSo the review and the template review now disagree.\n\nIn the backlog:\n\n- The inc-156 AC (backlog.json:900) explicitly asks for '4. Consignment addresses'.\n- Its note (line 963) says the heading follows inc-101 once it lands.\n- inc-158 (line 1085) says the review's own sections, renumbered.\n\nThese template review heading checks all pin the stale wording, so they hide the gap:\n\n- check-answers.test.js:2750\n- check-answers.fit.spec.js:1522\n- template-page.fit.spec.js:31\n\nThe template task list (inc-155, backlog.json:778/786) rightly stays on 'Consignment parties'. It follows the hub's captions.\n\nThe merge introduced this. The merge kept the frozen copy instead of deriving the heading.","area":"The templates merge resolutions","priority":1,"ranks":[3],"also":[],"rank":3},{"id":"workspace-tooling-laptop2-GATE-3","title":"The gate can still tear down the run's own lease, and stackAfterLocal makes the window for it bigger","repo":"trade-imports-workspace","file":"tim/src/build/gate.js","line":438,"what":"None of the five commits changes takeLease or giveBack.\n\n- acquireDecision still returns 'start' for the same holder when the stack is down.\n- runE2e still calls giveBack whenever lease.started.\n\nA gate run under the run's holder may find the run's stack down. If so, it starts the stack, then stops it and clears the run's lease.\n\n0ff6e2f4 adds `if (context.stackAfterLocal) await context.localDone` before takeLease. gates.json now sets stackAfterLocal true.\n\nSo the lease is checked only after layer one. With parallelRepos 1, layer one can take well over an hour.","scenario":"The run holds the lease and the stack is up.\n\nDuring the ladder's 90-minute one-repo-at-a-time layer one, Docker Desktop restarts. The stack goes down.\n\nBefore these commits, the gate checked the lease when it started. Now it checks only after layer one. Then it:\n\n- finds its own holder with the stack down\n- starts the stack (started=true)\n- runs E2E\n- releases: it stops the stack and clears the run's lease\n\nThe next stage's lease status shows no lease. Then either:\n\n- it returns stackHeld with holder null, which stops the loop\n- or the run's own release at the end finds nothing to release","severity":"medium","category":"correctness","fix":"In runE2e, the holder may be a build-run holder that already had a lease. (The lease existed for this holder before acquire.) In that case, never give it back:\n\n- leave the stack up and the lease in place\n- report startedForE2e so the loop knows\n\nOr refuse with a 'stackDown' result, so recoverDownStack takes the lease again properly.\n\nAdd a gate.test case:\n\n- a lease held by the same holder\n- the stack down\n- phase all\n\nAssert that the lease file still names the holder. Assert that stop-stack.sh was not called.","verifierNote":"- stack-lease.js acquireDecision line 146: for the same holder, `if (!up) return 'start'`.\n- acquireStack then calls startUnderLease (started:true).\n- gate.js:453 calls giveBack whenever lease.started. That stops the stack and clears the run's own lease.\n\nThe bug is older than these commits. None of them changes it.\n\nThese three together move the lease check to after a long one-repo-at-a-time layer one:\n\n- gate.js:438 (`if (context.stackAfterLocal) await context.localDone`)\n- gates.json stackAfterLocal true\n- parallelRepos 1\n\nSo the stack has more time to go down unseen.\n\nThe likely outcome is that the loop stops on stackHeld with holder null.\n\nCode under test is not mistaken for green.","area":"Workspace tooling changed on laptop 2","priority":1,"ranks":[4],"also":[],"rank":4},{"id":"tests-templates-F6","title":"Cross-service templates specs run on CDP, while the matching navigation specs are compose-only","repo":"trade-imports-ins-tests","file":"tests/ins/e2e/features/templates-navigation.spec.ts","line":5,"what":"These specs are tagged @compose:\n\n- INS templates-navigation\n- INS use-template\n- plants templates-navigation\n\n`npm test` (CDP) excludes them. This follows the precedent of address-book-navigation and cross-service-sessions.\n\nBut these specs cross services too:\n\n- Every animals templates spec crosses animals to INS (the type question) and back.\n- notification-type.spec crosses INS to animals and plants.\n\nNone of those is tagged @compose, so they all run on CDP.\n\nSo one of these is true:\n\n- The cross-service hop works on CDP, and three specs lose CDP coverage for no reason.\n- It does not work, and about 60 templates and type-question tests will fail on CDP.\n\nSam's rule is that a test behaves the same on both environments.","scenario":"A CDP run (`npm test`) executes create-template's beforeEach. Create new template leaves the animals origin for INS's /notification-type/template.\n\nThe cross-service session hop is what keeps the navigation specs compose-only.\n\n- If that hop fails on CDP, every templates spec fails in beforeEach.\n- If it works, the Templates and Use template links that start the templates flow from INS and plants are never checked on CDP.","severity":"medium","category":"environment-parity","fix":"Decide one way for the cross-service hop. Either:\n\n- remove @compose from the three navigation specs, since the templates specs show the hop is expected to work on CDP\n- or confirm on CDP that the hop fails, and tag the templates and type-question specs to match\n\nRecord the reason either way.","verifierNote":"The tag split is inconsistent.\n\nThese specs are tagged @compose, so `npm test` (grep-invert '@compose') skips them on CDP:\n\n- INS templates-navigation\n- INS use-template\n- plants templates-navigation\n\nThey were added in this merge, in commits 17038b3 and b9adef9.\n\nMeanwhile these specs are tagged only @integration:\n\n- create-template, save-template, template-review and the other animals templates specs\n- the animals templates-navigation spec\n- notification-type.spec\n\nThey make the same animals-to-INS or INS-to-animals/plants hop. They use the same waitForURL-on-origin-change plus completeSignInIfRequested pattern.\n\nNeither the merge log nor the ruling gives a reason for the difference.\n\nThe existing precedent (address-book-navigation, cross-service-sessions) is also unexplained.\n\nOne of the two groups is tagged wrongly for CDP.","area":"Tests repo: templates specs","priority":1,"ranks":[5],"also":[],"rank":5},{"id":"tests-templates-F5","title":"'Listed first' assertions race with other workers saving templates as the same user","repo":"trade-imports-ins-tests","file":"tests/animals/e2e/features/templates/save-template.spec.ts","line":102,"what":"Same cause as rank 1.\n\nLines 68, 102 and 103 assert that the template this test just saved is the first card on Manage templates.\n\n- Andrew's template list is shared by every worker.\n- Manage templates has no pagination, so it lists everything, newest first.\n\nAnother worker's save may commit between this test's save and the Manage templates render. If so, that save takes first place.\n\nThe 'Untitled template' case (line 103) is worse. Several workers save untitled templates, so this test cannot tell which one is its own.","scenario":"Workers 2 and 5 press Save template within the same second.\n\n- Worker 2's redirect renders after worker 5's save, so worker 5's card is first.\n- firstCardViewLink's href holds worker 5's template id.\n- Worker 2's test fails.","severity":"medium","category":"flake","fix":"Do one of these:\n\n- Run the save-template 'from a signed-in session' block as a dedicated cold identity.\n- Or assert the order relative to a template this test saved earlier, as the 'of two templates' test does.\n\nAlso give names a crypto.randomUUID() suffix rather than Date.now() in:\n\n- save-template line 48\n- view-template line 54\n- template-review\n\nchange-template and use-template already do this.\n\ntemplateCard(name) uses exact matching. So a same-millisecond collision between workers breaks strict mode.","verifierNote":"save-template.spec.ts:68, 102 and 103 assert either:\n\n- that the first card on Manage templates is this test's template\n- or that the first title is 'Untitled template'\n\nAndrew's template list is shared by every parallel worker signed in as defaultUser. Several specs added in this merge save templates as Andrew.\n\nA save that commits between this test's save and the Manage templates render takes first place.\n\nFor the untitled case, another worker's untitled save makes the test unable to tell which card is its own.\n\nThe 'of two templates' test (124-137) uses relative order and is safe.\n\nThe Date.now() name collision point is real but very unlikely.\n\nThe main race holds.","area":"Tests repo: templates specs","priority":1,"ranks":[6],"also":[],"rank":6},{"id":"tests-templates-F3","title":"'Never appears among the user's notifications' can never fail","repo":"trade-imports-ins-tests","file":"tests/animals/e2e/features/templates/save-template.spec.ts","line":145,"what":"The test looks for the template's name on the dashboard.\n\nDashboard cards show only:\n\n- a GBN-AG reference\n- commodity\n- origin\n- arrival\n- consignee\n- consignor\n- status\n\nNone of these can hold a template name.\n\nSo even if Save template wrongly created a draft notification, the name would still be absent. The assertion would pass.","scenario":"A regression makes saving a template also create a live-animals draft.\n\n- The new draft appears first on Andrew's dashboard with a GBN-AG reference.\n- getByText(name) still finds nothing.\n- The test stays green.","severity":"medium","category":"weak-assertion","fix":"Record requests to the animals origin across the save, as create-template's requestsToAnimals does.\n\nThen assert none went to /live-animals/start or /live-animals/notifications/*.\n\nThat holds up under parallel runs, unlike comparing the draft list (see F1).\n\nAlternatively, save as a dedicated cold identity and assert its dashboard has no drafts.","verifierNote":"save-template.spec.ts:139-146 asserts only that main contains no text matching the template name.\n\nIn this merge, the dashboard controller adds only a GET /start route.\n\nDashboard cards render reference, commodity, origin, arrival, parties and status. Nothing carries a template name.\n\nSo if saving a template wrongly created or listed a draft, the card would have no name in it. The assertion would still pass.\n\nThe check cannot fail on the regression its title names.","area":"Tests repo: templates specs","priority":1,"ranks":[7],"also":[],"rank":7},{"id":"tests-templates-F2","title":"'Requires the region of origin code' check passes even if that rule is removed","repo":"trade-imports-ins-tests","file":"tests/animals/e2e/features/templates/create-template.spec.ts","line":204,"what":"The test picks Yes for the region code but never chooses a country.\n\nIt then asserts only that:\n\n- an error summary shows\n- the title starts with 'Error:'\n\nTemplate mode keeps the page's required-answer checks (c-308). So a missing country alone produces both.\n\nNothing ties the error to the region code.","scenario":"Someone drops the 'region code required when Yes' check from template mode.\n\n- Save and continue still fails on the missing country.\n- The error summary still shows.\n- The test passes.\n\nThe behaviour its title promises is no longer tested.","severity":"medium","category":"weak-assertion","fix":"Call originOfImport.selectCountry('France') before choosing Yes, as the next test does.\n\nThen assert the exact region-code entry, as well as the 'Error:' title. Use either:\n\n- errorSummaryLinks.filter({ hasText: <region code required message> })\n- or originOfImport.errorLinkNamed(...)","verifierNote":"- In origin/validate.js the country is oneOf(countryValues).\n- In run.js a blank choice is an any.only missing answer. It is dropped only when allowMissing is true.\n- The origin controller passes allowMissing only for kit.isHubExit (Save and return).\n\nSo 'Save and continue' with no country produces a country error by itself.\n\nThe test at create-template.spec.ts:197-209 never picks a country. It asserts only that an error summary shows and the title starts with 'Error:'.\n\nIt still passes if regionCodeSuffixRule stops requiring the code under Yes.\n\nThe next test (211-227) shows the right pattern: select France and filter errorSummaryLinks by the exact message.","area":"Tests repo: templates specs","priority":1,"ranks":[8],"also":[],"rank":8},{"id":"templates-backend-BE-TPL-2","title":"The ownership boundary on PUT /details and PUT /fulfilments is only proven through a mocked repository","repo":"trade-imports-animals-backend","file":"src/test/java/uk/gov/defra/trade/imports/animals/integration/NotificationTemplateIT.java","line":96,"what":"The ITs test cross-user access only for:\n\n- GET /templates/{id}\n- POST /templates/{id}/save\n\nNothing over HTTP and Mongo shows that PUT /templates/{id}/details and PUT /templates/{id}/fulfilments:\n\n- return 404 for another user\n- leave the document unchanged\n\nNotificationTemplateServiceTest mocks NotificationTemplateRepository with Mockito. It stubs findByIdAndOwnerId and verifies save().\n\nThat tests which repository method is called rather than the behaviour. It also breaks the house rule to mock only at the network boundary.\n\nEither of these refactors would keep the unit tests green:\n\n- a refactor to findById plus an owner check\n- a refactor to a MongoTemplate update that drops the owner criterion","scenario":"Someone later swaps load() for repository.findById(id) in replaceFulfilments. For example, they do it to share code with a future 'use template' path.\n\nUser B can then overwrite user A's template answers by guessing or leaking a 24-hex id.\n\nThe unit test with stubbed findByIdAndOwnerId is rewritten alongside the change. No IT fails.\n\nThis is the same class of hole as the programme's open 'notifications listed unscoped per user' security ticket.","severity":"medium","category":"test-coverage","fix":"Add ITs for both PUT routes with OTHER_OWNER:\n\n- expect 404\n- then read the row from the repository\n- assert that setId, name and fulfilments are unchanged\n\nReplace the Mockito-repository service test with the IT. Or keep only the parts that need no repository mock.\n\nThe saved-time rules can be checked through the IT as well.","verifierNote":"Confirmed.\n\nNotificationTemplateIT has cross-owner tests only for:\n\n- GET /{id} (get_shouldReturn404_forAnotherOwnersTemplate)\n- POST /{id}/save (save_shouldReturn404_forAnotherOwnersTemplate)\n\nNo IT sends OTHER_OWNER to PUT /details or PUT /fulfilments.\n\nNotificationTemplateServiceTest uses a Mockito @Mock repository. It stubs findByIdAndOwnerId and verifies save(). That breaks the rule to mock only at the network boundary.\n\nToday's code is correct, because every path goes through load().\n\nThe gap is regression protection for the ownership boundary.","area":"Animals backend: templates (#106)","priority":1,"ranks":[9],"also":[],"rank":9},{"id":"shared-frontend-F1","title":"Add a new address from a template's party picker returns to a notification URL and 404s, losing the address","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"src/server/app/sets/live-animals/journeys/linear/features/addresses/party-picker/party-picker.controller.js","line":83,"what":"templateModeRoutes (src/server/app/shared/template-mode.js:123) twins every party picker under /templates.\n\nIn template mode, the picker still builds the INS add-address link with buildInsAddAddressUrl(request, h, journey.journeyId, party). Here journeyId is the template id (ins-handshake.js:48).\n\nINS always builds the return URL as <animals>/live-animals/notifications/{notification-id}/address-return (trade-imports-ins-frontend src/server/app/features/address-book/journey-registry.js:9).\n\nSo the return lands on the notification route, outside template mode. Then:\n\n- currentJourney calls the notification records port with a template id.\n- Templates live in the separate notification_templates collection, so the load misses.\n- Boom.notFound is thrown.\n\nThe /templates/{journeyId}/address-return twin exists, but nothing ever reaches it.","scenario":"Outside stub mode, the trader:\n\n- creates or changes a template\n- opens Roles and addresses, then Consignor, then 'Add a new address'\n- adds the address in INS\n- is redirected to /live-animals/notifications/<templateId>/address-return?..., which shows a 404\n\nThen:\n\n- The address never reaches the template.\n- The handshake token cookie is left behind.\n- The trader is off the template journey, with no way back except the nav.\n\ninc-161 makes party addresses part of a template, so this path is in scope.","severity":"medium","category":"correctness","fix":"Carry the mode through the handshake. Either:\n\n- add a return-kind parameter (for example 'return-to=template') that INS's journey-registry maps to /live-animals/templates/{id}/address-return\n- or have the animals frontend pass INS a full return path that INS allow-lists\n\nAs a stop-gap, hide the 'Add a new address' link while inTemplateMode().\n\nAdd a template-mode fit or E2E test for the add-address round trip. It would catch this with the stub off.","verifierNote":"Holds.\n\nTEMPLATE_MODE_EXCLUDED_SLUGS leaves out only documents, declaration, confirmation, amend, cancel-amend, copy and delete. So templateModeRoutes twins every party picker under /templates/{journeyId}/<party-slug>.\n\nparty-picker.controller.js:82-83 builds the link with buildInsAddAddressUrl(request, h, journey.journeyId, party) whenever stub mode is off. In template mode, journeyId is the template id.\n\nINS journey-registry.js:8-9 hard-codes the return as /notifications/{notification-id}/address-return. That route runs outside template mode. Then:\n\n- state.commit loads the id through the notification records port\n- it finds nothing\n- currentJourney throws Boom.notFound (engine/journey.js:129-131)\n\nNothing reaches the /templates/{journeyId}/address-return twin.\n\nThe merge introduced the twinning.\n\nI rated it below high because the new address is still saved in the org's address book. The trader can get back to the template from the Templates nav and pick it.\n\nThe template is not updated, though. The handshake cookie is left behind.","area":"Frontend shared code changed by templates","priority":2,"ranks":[10],"also":[],"rank":10},{"id":"shared-frontend-F2","title":"INS CSP form-action falls back to localhost:3007 and the env key is not in the cdp-app-config checkout","repo":"trade-imports-ins-frontend","file":"src/config/config.js","line":204,"what":"defraId.formActionOrigins defaults to ['http://localhost:3007']. content-security-policy.js:13 adds it to form-action.\n\nThe comment says it exists because:\n\n- the type question's form POST redirects to the animals or plants /start\n- that /start redirects again to Defra ID when that frontend has no session\n\nBrowsers check form-action on every redirect hop.\n\nThe local cdp-app-config checkout (services/trade-imports-ins-frontend/{dev,test}, last commit 28 Sep) has no DEFRA_ID_FORM_ACTION_ORIGINS.\n\nUnless it is set, CDP gets a form-action that allows localhost and not the real Defra ID origin.","scenario":"On CDP dev or test, a trader is signed in to INS but not yet to the animals frontend.\n\nThey choose 'Live animals' on 'What are you importing?' and press Continue.\n\n- The POST 302s to animals /live-animals/start.\n- That 302s to the Defra ID authorize URL.\n- The browser blocks the navigation under form-action.\n\nSo the trader sees nothing happen, or a blocked-navigation error.\n\nThe same applies to the plants types.\n\nProduction CSP also advertises http://localhost:3007.","severity":"medium","category":"deployment-config","fix":"Add DEFRA_ID_FORM_ACTION_ORIGINS (the browser-facing Defra ID origin) to every INS cdp-app-config environment.\n\nPer the CDP rule, draft it locally and Sam commits.\n\nConsider both of these, so a missing env value fails safe instead of shipping localhost:\n\n- defaulting to [] outside local development\n- setting the localhost value in docker/stack/frontend.compose.yml","verifierNote":"Holds.\n\nThe merge added defraId.formActionOrigins to INS config.js:204-209 with the default ['http://localhost:3007']. It spread it into CSP formAction (content-security-policy.js:31-35).\n\nThe notification-type controller answers a form POST with a redirect to the journey frontend's /start (controller.js:106).\n\nIf that frontend has no session, it redirects again to Defra ID. form-action is checked at each redirect hop.\n\nIn the local cdp-app-config checkout (last commit 28 Sep 2026), the INS dev env:\n\n- sets DEFRA_ID_OIDC_CONFIGURATION_URL to the trade-imports-defra-id-stub.dev.cdp-int.defra.cloud host\n- has no DEFRA_ID_FORM_ACTION_ORIGINS\n\nSo on CDP the policy allows localhost:3007 and not the stub or Defra ID origin.\n\nCheck the live cdp-app-config before acting.","area":"Frontend shared code changed by templates","priority":2,"ranks":[11],"also":[],"rank":11},{"id":"shared-frontend-F3","title":"Plants Templates nav item links to http://localhost:3000 unless TRADE_IMPORTS_ANIMALS_FRONTEND_URL is set in CDP","repo":"trade-imports-plants-frontend","file":"src/config/config.js","line":407,"what":"The new tradeImportsAnimalsFrontend.baseUrl defaults to http://localhost:3000.\n\ncontext.js builds templatesUrl from it for every plants page's service navigation.\n\nOnly the workspace stack sets TRADE_IMPORTS_ANIMALS_FRONTEND_URL (docker/stack/frontend.compose.yml:119).\n\nThe cdp-app-config checkout has it for the INS frontend, but not for services/trade-imports-plants-frontend.","scenario":"On CDP dev or test, any signed-in plants page shows 'Templates' in the service navigation.\n\nIt points at http://localhost:3000/live-animals/templates.\n\nClicking it fails to connect.","severity":"medium","category":"deployment-config","fix":"Add TRADE_IMPORTS_ANIMALS_FRONTEND_URL=https://trade-imports-animals-frontend.<env>.cdp-int.defra.cloud to each plants environment in cdp-app-config.\n\nSam commits it.\n\nShip the config before or with the merge.","verifierNote":"Holds.\n\nThe merge added tradeImportsAnimalsFrontend.baseUrl to plants config.js (default http://localhost:3000, env TRADE_IMPORTS_ANIMALS_FRONTEND_URL).\n\ncontext.js puts templatesUrl into every page's context. layout.njk:45 uses it as the Templates nav href.\n\nThe cdp-app-config checkout's plants dev env (a real deployment with auth enabled) has no TRADE_IMPORTS_ANIMALS_FRONTEND_URL.\n\nIt is also missing TRADE_IMPORTS_INS_FRONTEND_URL. So the plants env file is behind on browser-facing URLs generally. But the Templates link is a new break.\n\nThe checkout is from 28 Sep, so check the live repo.","area":"Frontend shared code changed by templates","priority":2,"ranks":[12],"also":[],"rank":12},{"id":"templates-features-TPL-1","title":"Template card category and title sit side by side, not stacked","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"src/server/app/sets/live-animals/journeys/linear/features/templates/template.njk","line":29,"what":"The card is hand-built. It puts these as two direct children of .govuk-summary-card__title-wrapper:\n\n- <p class=\"govuk-caption-m\"> (category)\n- <h3 class=\"govuk-summary-card__title\">\n\nIn the installed govuk-frontend, that wrapper is `display:flex; flex-wrap:nowrap; justify-content:space-between` from tablet width up.\n\nSo the two elements become flex items in one row:\n\n- 'Live animals' on the left\n- the template name pushed to the right\n\nThe design (dashboard-template-card.html) stacks the category above the title inside its own heading div.","scenario":"On any screen at tablet width or wider, open Manage templates with one saved template.\n\nThe card header reads 'Live animals ........ Weekly cattle' on one line. It does not show the caption above the name.\n\nThe FIT specs check roles and text, not layout. So nothing catches it.","severity":"medium","category":"govuk-toolbox / layout","fix":"Wrap the caption and the h3 in one plain <div> inside the title wrapper. Then the wrapper has a single flex child.\n\nThat mirrors the design's heading div and needs no custom CSS.","verifierNote":"- template.njk:29-34 puts the caption <p> and the <h3> as two direct children of .govuk-summary-card__title-wrapper.\n- The installed govuk-frontend (summary-list/_mixin.scss:203-216) makes that wrapper display:flex; flex-wrap:nowrap; justify-content:space-between from tablet up. So the two sit side by side.\n- No app stylesheet overrides the wrapper (only _summary-card-error.scss touches summary-card).\n- The design partial dashboard-template-card.html wraps both in a single __heading div.\n- copy.categories always gives 'Live animals' for live-animals templates, so every card is affected.\n\nThe templates merge added this file.","area":"Templates feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"ranks":[13],"also":[],"rank":13},{"id":"workspace-tooling-laptop2-GATE-2","title":"gate-wait cannot tell a dead gate from a slow one, so a crashed gate costs up to about 3 hours","repo":"trade-imports-workspace","file":"tim/src/build/gate-result.js","line":60,"what":"waitForGateResult only checks whether the result file exists.\n\nA gate killed by the OS or the tool writes no result. That covers:\n\n- SIGKILL from memory pressure on the 16 GB laptop these commits are about\n- a Bash tool that kills rather than backgrounds on timeout\n\nThe catch block that writes an error envelope runs only for thrown errors, not for signals.","scenario":"A five-repo baseline's mvn verify and FIT suites push the laptop into memory pressure. The tim node process is killed.\n\nThe agent gets GATE_STILL_RUNNING 20 times, about 3 hours, before reporting \"the gate did not finish\".\n\nMeanwhile no gate is running.\n\nIf it was killed after the stack started, the stack is left up under the lease.","severity":"low","category":"reliability","fix":"Have the gate write a marker with its pid when it starts (the same marker as GATE-1).\n\nOn each poll, have gate-wait:\n\n- check that the pid is still alive (process.kill(pid, 0))\n- return a distinct GATE_DIED error at once when the process has gone and there is no result\n\nAdd a test that spawns a gate, kills it, and asserts that gate-wait reports it dead instead of still running.","verifierNote":"waitForGateResult only polls for the file.\n\nNothing in gate-result.js or index.js records a pid or checks that the gate process is alive.\n\nThe catch at index.js:343 runs only on thrown errors. So a SIGKILL of the tim node process writes nothing. gate-wait answers GATE_STILL_RUNNING until the agent's 20 waits are used up.\n\nI rate it low:\n\n- Memory pressure is more likely to kill the large java or chromium children than the tim node parent. tim sees those as a red rung.\n- The loop's rule also moves a timed-out call to the background instead of killing it.","area":"Workspace tooling changed on laptop 2","priority":1,"ranks":[14],"also":[],"rank":14},{"id":"templates-backend-BE-TPL-1","title":"Template updates are read-modify-write with a server-only version: a double-clicked Save returns 409 and two tabs silently overwrite each other","repo":"trade-imports-animals-backend","file":"src/main/java/uk/gov/defra/trade/imports/animals/template/NotificationTemplateService.java","line":110,"what":"updateDetails, replaceFulfilments and save each do the same three steps:\n\n- call load() (findByIdAndOwnerId)\n- change the entity\n- call repository.save()\n\n@Version is on the entity. But the frontend client (services/templates/client.js) never sends the version back.\n\nNo endpoint accepts an expected version.\n\nThe only concurrency check is whether two requests overlap inside the backend:\n\n- Overlapping requests get an OptimisticLockingFailureException. The global handler turns it into 409 STALE_CONCURRENCY_TOKEN.\n- Requests that do not overlap silently overwrite each other, because PUT /fulfilments replaces the whole list.","scenario":"(a) The user double-clicks 'Save template' on the review page.\n\n- Two POST /templates/{id}/save requests both load version 3.\n- The first writes version 4.\n- The second gets a 409. The frontend's failed() throws a BackendRequestError.\n- The user sees an error page, even though the template was saved.\n\n(b) The user opens a saved template in two tabs.\n\n- Tab A changes origin, then tab B changes the consignee.\n- Tab B's PUT /fulfilments sends the full answer list it read before A's change.\n- A's origin change is lost, and no error is shown.","severity":"low","category":"concurrency","fix":"Make save idempotent and atomic:\n\n- Use MongoTemplate.findAndModify with Criteria where(_id).is(id).and(ownerId).is(ownerId), and Update set(status, SAVED). A repeated save is then a no-op instead of a 409.\n- Set 'saved' with a separate conditional update (criteria saved: null) or $min.\n\nFor replaceFulfilments and updateDetails, accept the expected version (a concurrencyToken, as notifications use). Match on it, and return 409 when it is stale.\n\nHave the frontend send back the version it read. Have it handle the 409 as notifications already do.","verifierNote":"Holds on the branch.\n\nNotificationTemplateService.save, replaceFulfilments and updateDetails all call load() and then repository.save(). The entity carries @Version.\n\nThe frontend client (services/templates/client.js) sends no version. No endpoint accepts one.\n\nGlobalExceptionHandler maps OptimisticLockingFailureException to 409. check-answers saveTemplate (controller.js:286) has no catch. So an overlapping save shows an error page.\n\nThe template-records port sends the full fulfilment set it loaded earlier. So the two-tab lost update is real.\n\nThree things lower the severity:\n\n- Notifications' own fulfilment replace has no expected-version check either (only /copy takes a concurrencyToken). So this copies the house pattern rather than falling short of it.\n- A double-click gap (around 100ms) is usually longer than one Mongo find-and-save. So the second save normally runs after the first and leaves the template saved.\n- A lost update needs the same user editing in two tabs.","area":"Animals backend: templates (#106)","priority":1,"ranks":[15],"also":[],"rank":15},{"id":"merge-resolution-TPL-MERGE-3","title":"GET /start creates a draft notification on any signed-in request (outside the conflict hunks, merged into the combined dashboard controller)","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"src/server/app/sets/live-animals/journeys/linear/features/dashboard/controller.js","line":131,"what":"`startGet` is registered on `GET /start` and runs `openNewNotification`.\n\nThat calls `startJourney`, which creates and persists a draft through `records.create`, then `beginOpeningRun`.\n\nNo crumb or confirmation step is involved.\n\nThe backlog's own rule is that every state change is a form post. This is c-309, restated in the notes of inc-157, inc-158 and inc-160.\n\nThis route breaks that rule so that the INS type question can hand over cross-origin.\n\nPlants' dashboard gained the same route in the same merge.","scenario":"A trader is signed in. Then either:\n\n- they open any page that links to /live-animals/start\n- a link preview or prefetch fetches it\n\nLax cookies are sent on top-level GET navigation. So each fetch stores a new empty GBN-AG draft.\n\nThe draft then appears on the dashboard and needs deleting.","severity":"low","category":"security","fix":"Make /start idempotent and free of side effects. Either:\n\n- render a GOV.UK confirmation page with its own crumb'd form post that creates the draft\n- or have the INS type question hand over a short-lived signed token, and check it before creating anything\n\nApply the same change to plants' /start.","verifierNote":"The scenario holds.\n\ndashboard/controller.js:131-157 registers GET /start. It runs openNewNotification (startJourney followed by beginOpeningRun) and creates a draft with no crumb.\n\nThe merge added this route (it is not in HEAD^1).\n\nThe auth cookie is SameSite=Lax (config.js:19, auth.js:69). So a top-level cross-site GET or a prefetch carries the session and creates an empty draft.\n\nTwo caveats lower the severity:\n\n- It is a deliberate inc-148 design choice, documented in the code comment and backlog.json:182: a cross-origin POST cannot carry the crumb. It sat behind a human design gate (line 187).\n- The finding overstates c-309. In the backlog, c-309 covers storing and using templates through form posts, not every state change.\n\nThe only impact is an unwanted empty draft, with no data exposure.\n\nThis is not a merge-resolution defect, but it is real in the merged surface.","area":"The templates merge resolutions","priority":1,"ranks":[16,25],"also":[{"rank":25,"repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"src/server/app/sets/live-animals/journeys/linear/features/dashboard/controller.js","line":131,"what":"startGet is in the animals frontend and in the plants dashboard controller. On a plain GET, with no crumb or hand-off token, it:\n\n- creates a draft\n- adds it to the known-journeys cookie\n- begins the opening run\n\nThe 9 October afternoon ruling (ruling-sam-2026-10-09-pm.md:199) says every state change is a form post.\n\nA GET with side effects is also triggered by things that never meant to create anything.","scenario":"Each of these mints a new empty draft in the trader's list:\n\n- a link to <animals>/live-animals/start from any site. Top-level navigation carries the Lax or Strict same-site session cookie within cdp-int.defra.cloud.\n- browser prefetch or prerender of a typed or suggested URL\n- a link-preview bot running in the user's session\n- a refresh of /start\n\ninc-148's own AC says abandoning the question leaves no empty draft. These paths break that.","fix":"Choose one:\n\n- Have INS hand over with a short-lived signed hand-off token, as the address-book handshake does. INS mints an HMAC'd nonce. /start creates the draft only when the token verifies and has not been used.\n- Make /start render a one-button, crumb-protected 'Continue' form that POSTs to the existing create route.\n\nEither way, an unauthenticated-intent GET no longer writes.","verifierNote":"Holds.\n\nThe merge added startGet (animals dashboard/controller.js:131-132). On a plain GET with no crumb or token, it calls startJourney and beginOpeningRun, the same as createPost.\n\nThat is deliberate: a comment says a cross-origin POST cannot carry the crumb.\n\nA top-level navigation, a refresh, or a prerender of /start each creates a new empty draft owned by the signed-in trader.\n\nThat goes against the backlog AC that the question leaves no stray draft.\n\nThe 9 Oct pm ruling's 'every state change is a form post' is mainly about working without JavaScript. But it still applies.\n\nThe impact is nuisance drafts, not a data leak or a privilege problem, so I rated it low."}],"rank":16},{"id":"templates-backend-BE-TPL-6","title":"Dead 'legacy row' handling and migration-style wording for null-status rows that cannot exist; such a row also bypasses the in-progress guard","repo":"trade-imports-animals-backend","file":"src/main/java/uk/gov/defra/trade/imports/animals/template/NotificationTemplate.java","line":55,"what":"The whole template package arrives in this merge. No environment has notification_templates rows.\n\nEven so, the Javadoc and the code cater for rows from earlier increments on the same unmerged branch.\n\nThe Javadoc says:\n\n- 'Rows written before templates could be created carry none and read as saved'\n- 'or on a row saved before this was recorded'\n\nIn the code:\n\n- NotificationTemplateResponse.from treats a null status as SAVED\n- listFor's status-not-IN_PROGRESS filter lets null through\n- replaceFulfilments treats null as saved\n- the sort orders by saved then created to place legacy rows\n\nThat breaks the 'no migration comments' rule.\n\nIt also means any row inserted without a status is listed and usable as a saved template. For example, a row inserted by a raw seed, or by a future write path that forgets to set it.","scenario":"The IT seed() helper already inserts rows with no status. They appear on Manage templates as saved.\n\nA future write path might build a NotificationTemplate without .status(...). It would quietly publish a half-made template to the user's list, and let it start notifications.","severity":"low","category":"maintainability","fix":"- Make status required: give it a @Builder.Default of IN_PROGRESS and drop the null branches.\n- Have listFor query status == SAVED.\n- Sort by saved descending only, or by (saved, _id).\n- Remove the legacy wording from the Javadoc and the Schema descriptions.\n- Make the IT seed() set status SAVED and saved explicitly.","verifierNote":"Confirmed.\n\nThe whole template package is new in this merge (git log origin/main shows no history for it).\n\nNo seed in the tests repo or the frontend writes notification_templates. So no environment holds rows without a status.\n\nEven so:\n\n- The NotificationTemplate Javadoc (lines 55-56 and 61-64) describes rows 'written before templates could be created' and 'saved before this was recorded'.\n- The service Javadoc at line 17 says the same.\n- NotificationTemplateResponse.from maps a null status to SAVED.\n- The StatusNot(IN_PROGRESS) query lets a null status through.\n- The IT findAll_shouldStillListARowWithNoStatus seeds a 'Legacy' row to pin that behaviour.\n\nThis is migration-style wording and handling, which the house rules ban.\n\nIt also means a row written without a status shows up in the list as a saved template.","area":"Animals backend: templates (#106)","priority":1,"ranks":[17],"also":[],"rank":17},{"id":"templates-backend-BE-TPL-5","title":"GET /templates is unbounded and returns every template's full answers","repo":"trade-imports-animals-backend","file":"src/main/java/uk/gov/defra/trade/imports/animals/template/NotificationTemplateService.java","line":23,"what":"listFor loads every saved template for the owner, with no limit or paging.\n\nEach NotificationTemplateSummary carries the complete fulfilments list, so the frontend can summarise cards. That list is the whole notification answer payload.\n\nNothing limits:\n\n- the number of templates per user\n- the size of the fulfilments","scenario":"A high-volume importer keeps 200 templates. Each holds a full set of commodity, party and transport answers.\n\nEvery visit to Manage templates pulls all 200 documents and serialises them into one response. The frontend then decodes it.\n\nLatency and heap use grow linearly.\n\nUnder concurrent load, a large account can push the backend toward OOM.","severity":"low","category":"performance","fix":"Add paging, as notifications already have (page and size, plus totalItems from count).\n\nAlternatively, project only the fields the card needs (category, parties' addresses) on the server. Do not return the raw fulfilments.\n\nConsider a per-user cap on templates.","verifierNote":"Confirmed.\n\nlistFor calls findAllByOwnerIdAndStatusNotOrderBySavedDescCreatedDesc with no Pageable and no limit.\n\nNotificationTemplateSummary carries the full fulfilments. The frontend Manage templates page decodes every one through cardAnswersFor.\n\nNotifications have paging and templates do not.\n\nThe impact is speculative at expected per-user volumes, so the severity is low.","area":"Animals backend: templates (#106)","priority":1,"ranks":[18],"also":[],"rank":18},{"id":"templates-backend-BE-TPL-4","title":"POST /templates/{id}/save is an action path, not a noun endpoint","repo":"trade-imports-animals-backend","file":"src/main/java/uk/gov/defra/trade/imports/animals/template/NotificationTemplateController.java","line":118,"what":"The template API is new and could follow the house REST rule (noun endpoints).\n\n/details and /fulfilments are sub-resources, but /save is a verb.\n\n/save only changes a status field, so it fits the same sub-resource pattern.","scenario":"Delete and discard (inc-164 and inc-165) will be added beside this endpoint.\n\nThe verb style tends to spread into routes such as POST /templates/{id}/discard and POST /templates/{id}/delete.\n\nThen the API mixes styles, and needs a breaking change to tidy up.","severity":"low","category":"api-design","fix":"Model it as PUT /templates/{id}/status with body {\"status\":\"SAVED\"}.\n\nValidate it so it only accepts the transition from IN_PROGRESS to SAVED.\n\nUse DELETE /templates/{id} for the coming delete and discard rows.","verifierNote":"Confirmed at NotificationTemplateController.java:118.\n\n@PostMapping(\"/{id}/save\") is a verb path on a brand-new API, against the house rule for noun endpoints.\n\nIt is mitigated because the existing backend already has verb paths, such as POST /notifications/{ref}/copy.\n\nSo this is a style point, not a functional defect.","area":"Animals backend: templates (#106)","priority":1,"ranks":[19],"also":[],"rank":19},{"id":"workspace-tooling-laptop2-GATE-6","title":"Concurrency tests leave out the cap between 1 and N and the refresh path with a reused lease","repo":"trade-imports-workspace","file":"tim/src/build/gate.test.js","line":776,"what":"The new tests prove two things:\n\n- parallelRepos 1 runs strictly one repo at a time\n- stackAfterLocal starts a stack that was down only after the unit rung\n\nNothing covers a cap of 2 of 3 repos, where:\n\n- at most two overlap\n- the third starts once the first finishes\n- results are still in plan order\n\nThat is the setting ced1a1a3 shipped.\n\nNothing covers stackAfterLocal on the build loop's real path. There, the holder already leases the stack. So refreshLeasedStack, not run-stack.sh, is what has to wait for layer one.","scenario":"A later change breaks the 16 GB laptop protection.\n\nThe change could be to runChainsAtMost, for example:\n\n- a worker that takes its next chain before the last one finishes\n- limit handling that drops to all chains when limit > 1\n\nOr it could be a change to where refresh is called.\n\nEvery existing test still passes, and the baseline goes red on timeouts again.","severity":"low","category":"test-coverage","fix":"Add a timeline test with parallelRepos 2 and three repos. Assert that:\n\n- the largest number of starts without a matching end is 2\n- the results come back in plan order\n\nAdd a stackAfterLocal test with a lease seeded for the same holder and the stack up.\n\nAssert the rebuild or restart log entry in stack-calls comes after the unit rung's marker.","verifierNote":"- gate.test.js:793-821 tests only parallelRepos 1 (a strict sequence).\n- :823-847 tests stackAfterLocal only on the cold-start path (run-stack.sh -d then stop-stack.sh), with no lease seeded.\n\nNo test covers a cap between 1 and N (runChainsAtMost with a limit of 2 or more and fewer than all chains).\n\nNo test covers stackAfterLocal with a lease already held by the same holder. There, refreshLeasedStack is the call that has to wait.\n\nBoth gaps are real.","area":"Workspace tooling changed on laptop 2","priority":1,"ranks":[20],"also":[],"rank":20},{"id":"workspace-tooling-laptop2-GATE-5","title":"Help text, plain-text report and docs still say repos run at the same time and the stack gets ready during layer one","repo":"trade-imports-workspace","file":"tim/src/commands/build/index.js","line":301,"what":"gates.json now sets parallelRepos 1 and stackAfterLocal true. But:\n\n- The gate's --help still says \"The stack gets ready during the first layer\".\n- describeTiming prints \"unit and FIT first, repos at the same time\" in every plain-text report.\n- The runGate JSDoc and the comment above FLAKY_COUNT in gate.js say the same.\n- The README baseline row that 05ecdb98 edited still says \"unit, FIT and E2E side by side\".","scenario":"Someone reads a slow baseline's text report, or the README. They expect repos to run in parallel and the stack to come up early.\n\nThey look for a hang or starvation bug that is really the configured one-at-a-time order.\n\nReaders of the user-facing strings are told something that is no longer true.","severity":"low","category":"docs","fix":"Make describeTiming and the --help text depend on parallelRepos and stackAfterLocal.\n\nPut both in the outcome, so the report can say \"one repo at a time, stack readied after\".\n\nUpdate the runGate JSDoc and the README baseline row to match.","verifierNote":"The stale text is all there:\n\n- describeTiming at index.js:156 always prints 'unit and FIT first, repos at the same time'.\n- The --help at index.js:301 still says 'The stack gets ready during the first layer', without mentioning stackAfterLocal.\n- The comments at gate.js:371-373 and :487-490 and the runGate JSDoc at :546-550 say the stack gets ready during or beside layer one.\n\ngates.json now sets parallelRepos 1 and stackAfterLocal true.\n\nThe README line cited in the finding did not show up in tim/README.md. Its only 'side by side' match is unrelated. So that part is unverified.\n\nThis affects docs only.","area":"Workspace tooling changed on laptop 2","priority":1,"ranks":[21],"also":[],"rank":21},{"id":"merge-resolution-TPL-MERGE-2","title":"Template review unit test's 'no card errors' check is vacuous: it reads `card.errorMessage`, which no card has","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"src/server/app/sets/live-animals/journeys/linear/features/check-answers/check-answers.test.js","line":2725,"what":"The merged template-review describe asserts `cardsOf(context.sections).filter((card) => card.errorMessage)` equals `[]`.\n\nOn the combined markup (and already on the templates side):\n\n- a card's error is `card.error`, set by `withCardErrors` in incomplete-cards.js:164\n- a section's error is `section.error`\n\nNothing in check-answers sets `errorMessage`. So the filter always returns `[]`.\n\nThe template-mode part of `holdsSilently` in the merged controller suppresses card errors. It has no card-level unit coverage.\n\nOnly the `errorSummary` null check guards it.","scenario":"Remove `inTemplateMode() ||` from `holdsSilently` in check-answers/controller.js.\n\nAn unanswered template review then gets 'Complete origin of import' and similar errors on its cards.\n\nThe `errorSummary` toBeNull assertion catches that today. But the card-level assertion would pass either way.\n\nA later change that keeps the summary empty but marks cards would slip through.","severity":"low","category":"test-coverage","fix":"Assert on the real fields:\n\n- no card has `error`: `expect(cardsOf(context.sections).filter((card) => card.error)).toEqual([])`\n- no section has `error`: the same over `context.sections`\n\nThat makes the check fail when card errors return in template mode.","verifierNote":"I confirmed this one.\n\ncheck-answers.test.js:2725 filters cards on `card.errorMessage`. That field appears nowhere else in check-answers.\n\nCards carry `error` (incomplete-cards.js:161-164). So the filter always returns [], and the 'no card errors' half of the test is vacuous.\n\nThe suppression comes from `inTemplateMode()` in holdsSilently (controller.js:221). Only the errorSummary toBeNull assertion guards it.\n\nThe test came in with the templates surface (cab954f8), not a conflict hunk. But it is part of the merged diff.\n\nThis is a test-coverage gap only, not a runtime defect.","area":"The templates merge resolutions","priority":1,"ranks":[22],"also":[],"rank":22},{"id":"tests-templates-F7","title":"Origin template-mode absence checks miss a plain 'Save and return' button","repo":"trade-imports-ins-tests","file":"tests/animals/e2e/features/templates/create-template.spec.ts","line":148,"what":"originOfImport.btnSaveAndReturn matches 'Save and return to overview' by substring.\n\nChange mode renders a plain 'Save and return' button. toHaveCount(0) here and at template-review.spec.ts line 129 cannot see it.\n\nThe change-template spec checks the full button list with submitButtons. The create flow and the review's Change flow do not.","scenario":"Template creation's origin page starts showing the change-mode 'Save and return' button. For example, because the ?change flag leaks from the review's Change link.\n\nBoth absence checks still pass, because neither label contains 'Save and return to overview'.","severity":"low","category":"weak-assertion","fix":"Assert the whole button set, as change-template does.\n\nIn both places, use: expect(originOfImport.submitButtons).toHaveText(['Save and continue'])","verifierNote":"In origin-of-import-page.ts, btnSaveAndReturn is getByRole('button', { name: 'Save and return to overview' }). That is a substring and case-insensitive match.\n\nA bare 'Save and return' button does not contain that string. So toHaveCount(0) at create-template.spec.ts:148 and template-review.spec.ts:129 cannot detect it.\n\nThe same merge added btnSaveAndReturnToTemplate (exact) and submitButtons.\n\nThe contact-address side of template-review already checks btnSaveAndReturnBare at count 0. So the origin checks are the gap.\n\nLow impact.","area":"Tests repo: templates specs","priority":1,"ranks":[23],"also":[],"rank":23},{"id":"tests-templates-F8","title":"Germinal hand-off test pins the /start URL and passes on a 404","repo":"trade-imports-ins-tests","file":"tests/ins/e2e/features/notification-type/notification-type.spec.ts","line":160,"what":"The animals frontend has no /germinal-products route yet.\n\nThe ruling (inc-148 gate) allows the link to land before the set exists. So today the test passes on a 404 page.\n\nIt checks only the landed pathname, with a non-retrying expect.\n\nWhen the germinal set is built and /start creates a draft and redirects to its first page:\n\n- the landed path will no longer be /start\n- the test will fail, even though the hand-off works","scenario":"The germinal theme ships GET /germinal-products/start. It creates a draft and redirects to /germinal-products/notifications/<id>/origin.\n\ncontinueToService resolves on the final URL. So the toBe(`/germinal-products/start`) check fails.","severity":"low","category":"brittle-assertion","fix":"Capture the hand-off request instead. Wait for a request on the animals origin whose pathname is `${SET_BASES.germinalProducts}/start`.\n\nKeep the check that no /live-animals/ request was made.\n\nDo this before the germinal set lands, so the test holds up when it does.","verifierNote":"No germinal route exists in the animals frontend source. grep finds only copy and test references.\n\nSo today the hand-off lands on a 404. notification-type.spec.ts:158-160 passes on the URL alone, with plain non-retrying expects.\n\ncontinueToService waits only for the origin change and then for load. So the URL read is the final one after any redirect.\n\nThe live-animals precedent in this merge (dashboard startGet) creates a draft and redirects to the origin page.\n\nWhen germinal follows that shape, the toBe('/germinal-products/start') check will fail, even though the hand-off works.\n\nThis is brittle but correct today, so the severity is low.","area":"Tests repo: templates specs","priority":1,"ranks":[24],"also":[],"rank":24},{"id":"shared-frontend-F6","title":"No template-mode page is ever audited by Lighthouse","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"scripts/lighthouse/audit-targets.js","line":8,"what":"audit-targets builds its targets from features/index.js allRoutes.\n\nThe template-mode twins are:\n\n- /templates/{journeyId}, which is the template task list and the saved template page\n- every /templates/{journeyId}/<slug>\n\nThey are generated only in routes-live-animals.js by templateModeRoutes. So they are neither audited nor listed in SKIPPED.\n\nThe 'stale reason fails the build' guard cannot see them either.","scenario":"An accessibility regression on the saved template page or the template review passes the Lighthouse gate. For example:\n\n- a duplicate h1\n- an unlabelled Change link\n- the 'Template' strip\n\nIt passes because only /templates, /templates/new and the notification pages are audited.","severity":"low","category":"test-coverage","fix":"Feed auditPaths the same route list the server registers: allRoutes plus templateModeRoutes(allRoutes, {excludedSlugs}).\n\nIn the Lighthouse setup:\n\n- seed a saved template and an in-progress template\n- map /templates/{journeyId}/* to those ids (a FILLED_BY-style map keyed to 'template')\n\nTwins that are not worth auditing go into SKIPPED with reasons.","verifierNote":"Holds.\n\nscripts/lighthouse/audit-targets.js builds auditPaths and assertTargetsAreCurrent from allRoutes (features/index.js).\n\nallRoutes includes the templates controller's own routes (/templates, /templates/new, and /templates/{journeyId}/name in SKIPPED). It does not include the twins.\n\nThose are generated only in routes-live-animals.js:136-140 through templateModeRoutes.\n\nSo these are neither audited nor listed in SKIPPED:\n\n- the saved-template page\n- the template task list\n- every template-mode page twin\n\nThe stale-skip guard cannot see them either.","area":"Frontend shared code changed by templates","priority":2,"ranks":[26],"also":[],"rank":26},{"id":"templates-features-TPL-2","title":"Template unit tests spy on internal modules and one expectation is computed by the code under test","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"src/server/app/sets/live-animals/journeys/linear/features/templates/use-template.test.js","line":86,"what":"use-template.test.js stubs these with vi.spyOn:\n\n- templates.getFor\n- engine/journey.startJourney\n- flow/run-state.beginOpeningRun\n\nIt then asserts `startJourney` toHaveBeenCalledWith `{ fulfilment: notificationSeedFrom(decoded) }`. So the expected value comes from the production function itself.\n\nThe same pattern appears elsewhere:\n\n- controller.test.js:62 spies templates.listFor, and :128 spies countries.originDisplayLabel.\n- check-answers.test.js (template review block) spies templates.saveFor, and reads the session through the production helper takeSavedTemplate.\n- controller.test.js:225 asserts on the raw session key 'templateSaved'.\n\nNone of these are network-boundary mocks. Yet the templates service has a stub mode, and an HTTP client that nock could intercept.","scenario":"A regression in notificationSeedFrom changes both sides of the use-template assertion, so the test stays green. For example, the arrival date is no longer stripped.\n\nRenaming the session key or moving startJourney's signature breaks tests, while behaviour is unchanged.","severity":"low","category":"test-quality","fix":"Run these handlers against the templates stub (or nock the backend in real mode) and the in-memory store.\n\nAssert the resulting draft's stored answers and the redirect, not the calls to internal functions.\n\nFor the banner, assert that the next GET /templates renders the heading, not the session contents.","verifierNote":"The spies are real:\n\n- use-template.test.js:61-65 (templates.getFor, journey.startJourney, runState.beginOpeningRun)\n- controller.test.js:62/128/232 (templates.listFor, countries.originDisplayLabel)\n- the raw 'templateSaved' session key at controller.test.js:175-225\n- check-answers.test.js:2811/2828 (templates.saveFor)\n\nuse-template.test.js:86-88 does build its expected value with notificationSeedFrom.\n\nThe claimed impact is smaller than stated, though:\n\n- Line 90 also asserts a concrete value ('further-keeping').\n- seed-notification.test.js tests notificationSeedFrom directly.\n- use-template.fit.spec.js drives the real UI.\n\nSo a seeding regression would not go unnoticed.\n\nWhat remains is a house-rule breach (mocks below the network boundary).\n\nThe codebase already does this in places. For example, check-answers.test.js:488 spying on records.load before the merge.","area":"Templates feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"ranks":[27],"also":[],"rank":27},{"id":"templates-features-TPL-3","title":"Saved banner is taken from the session before the cards are built","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"src/server/app/sets/live-animals/journeys/linear/features/templates/controller.js","line":60,"what":"takeSavedTemplate clears the session key at line 60.\n\n`Promise.all(saved.map(toCard))` runs afterwards, at line 70. It awaits countries.originDisplayLabel, which loads reference data.\n\nThe comment above line 60 claims the banner survives a failed render. That only holds for a failed list call.","scenario":"A user saves a template and is redirected to Manage templates.\n\nThe list call succeeds. But the countries reference data fails to load on a cold cache, so the page errors.\n\nThe banner has already been cleared. So on the next visit, the user never sees the 'saved to templates' confirmation.","severity":"low","category":"correctness","fix":"Build templateCards before calling takeSavedTemplate (or take it last, just before h.view).\n\nThen drop the comment, because the ordering shows the intent.","verifierNote":"controller.js:60 calls takeSavedTemplate, which runs yar.clear. That happens before line 70 awaits Promise.all(saved.map(toCard)).\n\ntoCard goes through cardAnswersFor and then countries.originDisplayLabel.\n\nOutside stub mode, ensureLoaded throws Boom.serverUnavailable when reference data fails to load.\n\nyar still saves the cleared session on an error response. So the one-time banner is lost.\n\nThe comment at lines 58-59 only covers the list call failing.\n\nThis needs a real-mode reference-data failure at the exact moment after a save, so it is low.","area":"Templates feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"ranks":[28],"also":[],"rank":28},{"id":"templates-features-TPL-5","title":"New explanatory comments added against house rule","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"src/server/app/sets/live-animals/journeys/linear/features/check-answers/controller.js","line":283,"what":"The merge adds explanatory comments in several places.\n\nIn trade-imports-animals-frontend:\n\n- check-answers/controller.js:218-219 and :283-285\n- check-answers/copy/copy.en.js:160-163\n- check-answers/view-model/index.js (the 'A template's review leaves out…' addition)\n- templates/controller.js:58-59\n- templates/use-template.js:12-13\n- the header comment in _review-sections.njk\n\nIn trade-imports-ins-frontend:\n\n- notification-type/controller.js:25-26 and :49-51","scenario":"These comments drift from the code.\n\nThe controller.js:58 comment already overstates what the ordering protects (see TPL-3).","severity":"low","category":"style / house rules","fix":"Delete them.\n\nPut rationale in docs/ or the commit message. Let names such as `usable` and `saveTemplate` carry intent.","verifierNote":"The diff confirms the merge added these explanatory comments:\n\n- check-answers/controller.js: the holdsSilently comment at about line 218, and the saveTemplate comment at about 283\n- copy.en.js: the template block comment\n- view-model/index.js: the added sentence\n- templates/controller.js:58-59\n- use-template.js:12-13\n- INS notification-type/controller.js:25-26 and :49-51\n\nThis goes against the 'remove explanatory comments aggressively' rule.\n\nThe codebase already carries many such comments, so this is style only.","area":"Templates feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"ranks":[29],"also":[],"rank":29}],"killed":[{"title":"Ownership rests entirely on a caller-asserted User-Id header, and organisationId is stored but never used","area":"Animals backend: templates (#106)","severity":"medium","refutation":"Refuted as a defect in this merge.\n\n(1) The 'undefined' owner scenario cannot happen.\n\n- services/templates/index.js requireOwner() throws when ownerId is falsy.\n- It does this before every client call (listFor, startFor, getFor, updateDetailsFor, replaceFulfilmentsFor, saveFor).\n- So the literal string 'undefined' is never sent.\n\n(2) A trusted User-Id header is the backend's existing model.\n\n- NotificationController already takes HEADER_USER_ID as a required header at lines 225 and 258, without verifying it.\n- The templates merge did not introduce it.\n\n(3) Scoping templates by user, not organisation, is the stated intent.\n\n- The backlog constraint (backlog.json line 39) reads 'scoped to the signed-in user's organisation (or user, for templates)'.\n- The field's Javadoc says organisationId is 'Recorded, not used to scope'.\n\nThe multi-organisation case is a product question, not a bug."},{"title":"An argument or workspace-lookup error no longer writes, or clears, the --result file","area":"Workspace tooling changed on laptop 2","severity":"low","refutation":"The regression is real.\n\nBefore 664beddd, `resultPath = opts.result ? resolve(opts.result) : null` came before parseOptions. So a parse or workspace-lookup error wrote an error envelope.\n\nNow index.js:309-328 sets resultPath only after both succeed.\n\nThe harmful scenario does not hold, though:\n\n- Those errors make the foreground gate call exit 2 at once, with the error on stdout.\n- It is never moved to the background, so GATE_WAIT_RULE never sends the agent to gate-wait.\n- GATE_RULE (increment-build-loop.js:982-983) says an error before any rung is RED.\n- The holder is always the fixed RUN_HOLDER from gateCommand. So the 'regex refuses the holder' trigger cannot come from the loop.\n- Even before the commit, a parse failure never cleared the file.\n\nTo read a stale envelope, an agent would have to break the loop's rules."},{"title":"Retargeted 'no uploaded documents' check (Document 1 card) can never fail","area":"Tests repo: templates specs","severity":"low","refutation":"Refuted.\n\nview-model/cards/documents.js always renders one 'Document 1' card when nothing is uploaded ('with none uploaded it carries the design's single \"Document 1\" card reading Not applicable').\n\ndocumentsSection is unconditional.\n\nThe upload-health-certificate error applies only to SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED journeys, never to a template.\n\nSo if asTemplateReview (template-review.js) stopped filtering DOCUMENTS_SECTION_ANCHOR:\n\n- a 'Document 1' card would render\n- template-review.spec.ts:82 would fail\n\nThe merge log says this retarget was chosen for exactly that reason.\n\nOne side note does hold:\n\n- cardTitled('Identification details') at line 101 can never fail.\n- That is because Identification details is the species block's identifier table heading (species-block.js), not a .govuk-summary-card__title.\n- The heading-role check on line 102 is the one that can fail.\n\nThat is a minor point, not this finding."},{"title":"Choosing Germinal products for a template silently drops the trader back on Manage templates","area":"Frontend shared code changed by templates","severity":"low","refutation":"The code does behave as described. newPost and namePost redirect to templatesPath() for germinal-products (template-name/controller.js:75-77, 92-93).\n\nBut the theme backlog (line 739) says choosing Germinal products 'is observed here only as far as the template name page. What a germinal template asks after its name is req-1876's, in the germinal theme's row'.\n\nThe germinal set does not exist yet.\n\nWhat happens after the name page is unbuilt, out-of-scope behaviour. The brief says not to report that as a defect of the merge.\n\nIt is an interim placeholder, not a regression."},{"title":"Template saves drop the concurrency token: last write wins with no stale-action check","area":"Frontend shared code changed by templates","severity":"low","refutation":"The two-tab scenario does not happen.\n\nstate.commit (engine/write/commit.js:7) loads the current record fresh on every POST. It applies only that page's patch to it.\n\nSo tab B's Transport save merges onto the stored fulfilment, which already includes tab A's Origin change. It does not overwrite it with a stale copy.\n\nAlso, replaceJourneyFulfilment prefers the same-request memo (journey.js:147-150). So notification saves send the freshly loaded version, not the tab's rendered token. They would not reject this case either.\n\nWhat the template port drops is protection against a true simultaneous-write race. That is narrow, and not the scenario described."},{"title":"Check-answers reaches into the templates feature folder","area":"Templates feature folders (sample)","severity":"low","refutation":"Cross-feature imports between sibling feature folders were already common before the merge. At HEAD^1:\n\n- check-answers imports from ../addresses and ../documents\n- hub and notification-actions import from ../check-answers\n- declaration imports ../check-answers/refusal.js\n- contact imports ../addresses\n- dashboard imports ../origin/page.js\n\nSam's 'feature folders independent by design' ruling forbids extracting shared controllers to remove duplication. It does not ban one feature importing a helper from another.\n\nThe new imports follow the existing pattern. So this does not break a house rule the merge introduced."},{"title":"Germinal template's Use template button does nothing visible","area":"Templates feature folders (sample)","severity":"low","refutation":"A germinal template cannot be created today.\n\nINS does offer Germinal products in template mode, and hands over to /templates/new?type=germinal-products. But in template-name/controller.js:\n\n- newPost (lines 74-77) redirects to Manage templates before templates.startFor when type is germinal-products\n- namePost (lines 91-94) refuses to update an existing template to germinal\n\nNo germinal template is ever stored. So no card shows a dead Use template button.\n\nThe scenario ('chooses Germinal products, saves it, then presses Use template') cannot happen.\n\nThe setId check in use-template.js is defensive and is tested.\n\nChoosing Germinal does silently bounce back to the list. But that is a different, known deferral (req-1876), and not this finding."}]},"copy":{"lede":"A risk-ranked review of the Design Release 2.1 combined branch, <span class=\"mono\">feat/NO_JIRA-design-release-2-1-combined</span>.\n\nThe branch carries 8 draft PRs.\n\nThe page covers two reviews:\n\n- the six themes\n- the type question and templates (EUDPA-690), merged on 12 October and reviewed in its own section below the first findings","q-cdp-needed":"<strong>No.</strong>\n\nEvery connect timeout in the build's logs is to Docker Desktop's host-gateway address for <span class=\"mono\">host.docker.internal</span>, on:\n\n- port 8085 (animals backend)\n- port 8091 (plants backend)\n\nThere is no CDP evidence.\n\nNo log exists from after the fix either. So whether it fixed anything locally is unproven.","q-http-proxy":"It does not bypass the proxy.\n\nWith <span class=\"mono\">HTTP_PROXY</span> set, backend calls use built-in fetch and the global proxy agent, as before. That also means:\n\n- the 2 second bound never applies in CDP\n- the retry fires only if the socket to the proxy itself fails\n\nA backend the proxy cannot reach comes back as a 5xx or a tunnelling error. Neither is retried.\n\nIn CDP the change is effectively dead code.\n\nIt also puts CDP on a different fetch and FormData path from every test environment (finding rank 5).\n\n<strong>Retry safety:</strong> connect timeout and connection refused happen before any bytes are sent. So a create, submit or upload cannot be duplicated.\n\n<span class=\"mono\">ETIMEDOUT</span> is the exception (finding rank 25).","q-should-it-exist":"<strong>Verdict: remove it from production code in both frontends.</strong>\n\n- Revert <span class=\"mono\">backendFetch</span> to plain fetch.\n- Delete <span class=\"mono\">test/setup-backend-fetch.js</span>, <span class=\"mono\">backend-fetch.real.test.js</span> and the retry unit tests.\n- Keep <span class=\"mono\">stackWithCauses</span> error logging. It is a real diagnostic gain on its own.\n\nIf you want a local bound:\n\n- put it on the global dispatcher behind a stack-only flag\n- retry only on connect-refused and connect-timeout\n- ideally retry only for idempotent methods\n- test it against real sockets\n\n<strong>Verifier's correction:</strong> the retry decision is unit-tested.\n\n<span class=\"mono\">backend-fetch.test.js:73-108</span> fails if the retry branch is removed.\n\nWhat no test covers is the 2 second bound and the agent.","q-fit-timeout":"<strong>No.</strong>\n\nThe suite grew:\n\n- from 455 to 529 tests\n- from 12.7 to 18.0 minutes\n\nIt had 0 flaky and 0 retries.\n\nThe raise absorbs set-up cost. One-page assertions about submitted or amending notifications each drive a full journey in <span class=\"mono\">beforeEach</span>.\n\nMain was already using 85% of the 900s budget.\n\nSee finding rank 6.","q-flake-fixes":"<strong>Date checks widened to accept yesterday: these do hide a real product bug.</strong>\n\n- The plants frontend runs with <span class=\"mono\">TZ=Europe/London</span>.\n- It parses the backend's zone-less date-time as London time.\n- The confirmation, Date submitted and Date created pages show the previous calendar day between 00:00 and 01:00 BST.\n- See finding rank 3.\n\n<strong>test.slow() additions:</strong>\n\n- Each is a one-line addition with no step or assertion removed.\n- Each task now saves back to the Overview, which adds two page loads per task.\n\n<strong>Dashboard pagination seeded:</strong>\n\n- 26 creates now go through the frontend form client instead of 26 button clicks.\n- Every assertion is unchanged.\n- This is set-up for a one-page assertion, which the rule allows.\n\n<strong>Address-book beforeEach reorder:</strong>\n\n- Skip first, walk after.\n- Steps and all four assertions per test are identical to main.","q-leaked-commit":"Superseded by <span class=\"mono\">02dd6bc</span>. That commit landed the same document-card test with alphanumeric references and an existing JPG fixture.\n\nNothing in the leaked commit is needed.\n\nThe branch is safe to delete (finding rank 24).\n\nSide note:\n\n- The backend rejects non-alphanumeric document references, but the animals frontend has no matching format rule.\n- A trader typing <span class=\"mono\">UK-123</span> would get a backend 400 rather than a page error.\n- Not yet verified beyond the file-type check running first.","questionsTemplatesNote":"Type question and templates: the shared-user flake (templates rank 1) is being fixed on the templates branch.\n\nThe fix uses an isolated stub user per order-sensitive spec.","templatesSectionNote":"The severity, priority and area filters above apply to this section too.\n\nVerdicts work the same way.\n\nRanks here are separate from the ranks above, so they are shown as templates ranks.","footerHow":"How it was made:\n\n- 11 Opus reviewers, one per area\n- one adversarial Opus verifier per area, told to refute by default\n- a Sonnet pass ranked what survived\n- 23 agents in all\n\nThe type question and templates section was reviewed the same way, in six areas, after the merge.","verdictsReadyNote":"Choose a verdict on any finding. It saves as soon as you choose.\n\nComments save when you select 'Save comment' or leave the box.\n\nEveryone with access sees the verdicts."}};
